{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PNffXJDQEFLrNx1dDAXZmh","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (90.7KB). Full output saved to: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-024328--x54facc-work/9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae/tool-results/bsy4z2ug0.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# AGENTS.md: weetare\n\nweetare is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\nWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\nis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n\nBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\nit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\nthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n\n## Set up (once per machine)\n\n    bash scripts/setup.sh\n\nIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n   labs/game/main.ts.\n2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n\n`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae","uuid":"44053f5d-52d0-47fa-bac4-dd3972182f02","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:44:11.711Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# AGENTS.md: weetare\n\nweetare is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\nWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\nis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n\nBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\nit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\nthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n\n## Set up (once per machine)\n\n    bash scripts/setup.sh\n\nIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n   labs/game/main.ts.\n2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n\n`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n  configs and the docs it shipped: engine-lock.json lists every one, and `npm run check` fails when one changes. When\n  the engine is in your way, work round it in the game and write what you needed in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. Only when no\n  workaround exists, patch the engine file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" there, with why.\n- **Only the public API.** Pages import engine/index.ts; sim-side code imports engine/sim-api.ts (lint enforces it).\n- **Reproducible play.** Sim-side code reads no clock, no `Math.random`, no DOM: randomness comes from named streams\n  (`w.rng('loot')`), time is whole 60 Hz steps. The same intents and seed give the same hashes, in Node and Chromium.\n- **Assets are code or text first**: geometry, textures, materials and sounds as data. A binary file only with the\n  owner's approval, listed in APPROVED-BINARIES.json with their answer (`npm run check` fails one unlisted); fonts are\n  approved, anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them.\n- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines.\n- **Ask the owner** before anything outward or costly: a binary asset, a new npm dependency, spending money,\n  publishing. No answer within 15 minutes: take the safer choice, note it in labs/game/README.md, carry on.\n- Update the engine only when the owner asks: `node x engine --update`.\n\n## Done means\n\n`npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the bot still wins (teach it each new rule); a change that alters play on\npurpose records the replay's goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) with the reason\nin the commit; labs/game/README.md says what the game is now.\n# Making a game with my-3dge\n\nYou are making a game with the my-3dge engine. Read this once, start to finish; after that, look things up (section\n8). A game's AGENTS.md sends you here after `node tools/game/new-game.mjs` made the game. For a model without the\nrepository, this one file is also the engine in brief: its public API by area is section 10.\n\n## 1. What you have\n\nmy-3dge is a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier physics, the\nUI in HTML/CSS. Three promises shape everything you write:\n\n- **Play is reproducible.** The rules (the _sim_) run on the CPU at a fixed 60 steps a second. The same seed and the\n  same intents give the same state, bit for bit, in Node and in Chromium; every state has a 16-digit hash.\n- **Nothing needs a screen.** The sim runs headless in Node (tests, `node x sim`). The page renders on WebGPU in a\n  headless Chromium (`node x shot`) and reports what it drew as numbers: which object covers which pixels (the ID\n  pass), the look's metrics, a text thumbnail. You prove a game works with commands, not by looking.\n- **Code and data, not binary files.** Levels are text maps; materials and textures are generators with parameters;\n  sounds are voices and tracker notes; characters are procedural bodies, with a library of readable motion clips.\n\nA game's repository:\n\n| Path                                                             | What it is                                                            |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `labs/game/`                                                     | The game: the page, the rules (`scenes/`), the characters (`cast/`), the bot, the replays |\n| `labs/box/`                                                      | The engine's worked example: a hero, crowds, combat, props, looks, sound (read-only)   |\n| `engine/`                                                        | The engine. Game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts            |\n| `tools/`, `x.js`                                                 | The `node x` commands, the lint rules, the templates (tools/templates/)               |\n| `data/anim/`                                                     | The animation library: readable clips, and the engine's actions                       |\n| `docs/`                                                          | This guide, API.md (every public name) and ERRORS.md (every error code, with its fix) |\n| `engine-lock.json`, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`                         | The engine's pinned commit and file hashes; what you needed from the engine           |\n\nEverything but `labs/game/` and the root's game files (AGENTS.md, README.md, package.json…) is the engine's,\nread-only: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json.\n\n## 2. The model\n\n**Two halves.** The sim is the game's rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them each\nstep, events. Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts and never touches the\nDOM, the GPU, the clock or `Math.random`. The page (labs/game/main.ts) imports engine/index.ts: `createEngine` starts\nthe sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into intents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame`\nfor the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The page reads the sim and never writes it. Lint enforces the split.\n\n**A scene** is the unit `x sim`, the page and the tests run: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings,\nsetup(w), step(w, intents) })`. `setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. One page can\noffer several scenes (`createEngine({ scenes })`, `?scene=<id>` picks one); a running page cannot switch scenes yet,\nso a level select reloads the page with another `?scene`.\n\n**The world, `w`**, is what systems get:\n\n- **Components and entities.** `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields\n  with defaults and units, so the state can be hashed, captured and checked. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an\n  id; `w.query('gem')` returns the live entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)`, `w.add`, `w.remove` and\n  `w.despawn` do the rest. An entity whose component declares `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box until\n  something draws it properly (an actor, a prop, a crowd body).\n- **Systems** run in phases, in this order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`.\n  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` puts a rule after the physics moved the bodies and the\n  readback copied their places into components.\n- **Events.** `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)` listeners. A page\n  reads them from `frame.world.events.trace()` (sounds, effects); emitters burst on them; tests count them.\n- **Time** is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800), never by\n  adding up `w.dt`: floating-point sums drift. `w.timers` schedule in sim seconds.\n- **Randomness** comes from named streams seeded by the run: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n- **Settings** are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum,\n  unit, when: 'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`. Each is reachable from the URL\n  (`?game.gems=20`), from `node x sim … --set game.gems=20`, `node x set` and `__engine.set`, and replays record them.\n\n**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once per step, as plain data: `move` (a world-space\ndirection `[x, z]`), `b` (buttons held), `p` (pressed this step), `aim`, `look`, `cam`, and the game's own\n`'game:<name>'` keys. A player actor reads the step's intents itself; systems get them as their second argument\n(`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). In a page, devices make intents through an action map\n(`actionMap:thirdPerson` by default: WASD or the arrows run, Space jumps, Shift dashes); a map with `look: true` sends\nthe camera's view as `look`, and its `aimWith` fills `aim` (`centre`: what the screen's middle points at; `pointer`:\nthe pointer on the player's floor), and a game with several camera modes gives `createEngine` a map per camera\n(`actionMap: (code) => …`). A bot or a test writes them directly: `engine.step(1, { move: [1, 0] })`. A replay is the\nintents of a run, recorded.\n\n**Proof.** `engine.hash()` digests the whole sim. A replay file (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json) holds a scene,\nseed, settings, the intents at each step that changed them, and the golden hashes at its checkpoints, per platform\n(`linux-x64`). `capture()` and `restore()` copy a run and continue it exactly. If a hash changes, play changed.\n\n## 3. The starter, file by file\n\nThe starter (labs/game/) is a whole small game: run round an arena collecting every gem before the clock runs out.\nGrow your game from it; keep its tests as they are, and change what they check as the game changes.\n\n| File                         | What it shows                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.ts     | The rules: a level as text, settings, components (`gem`, `round`), systems, a random stream, events |\n| labs/game/cast/player.ts     | The player: a character on the controller (run, jump, dash) drawn as the humanoid          |\n| labs/game/bot.ts             | A bot that plays through intents, steering down a flow field to the nearest gem            |\n| labs/game/main.ts            | The page: `createEngine`, the HUD in `onFrame`, Enter to play again, Esc's pause menu, the chase camera     |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts | The tests: the golden replay, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds, the clock     |\n| labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json | The bot's play, recorded, with its golden hashes                                      |\n| labs/game/README.md          | The game's own page: what it is, how it plays, the calls made                              |\n\n## 4. What to reach for\n\nThe public names below are in engine/sim-api.ts (sim-side) or engine/index.ts (pages); section 10 lists them all.\nThe box (labs/box/, its README says which scene shows what) has a working use of almost each one.\n\n| You need                       | Reach for                                                                                 | See it in                          |\n| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| A level                        | A text map in a scene module; `compileLevel`, glyphs (`#` wall, `.` floor, `@` spawn, `w` low wall, `P` pillar, `^` stairs, `=` gallery, `o` dais, `t` brazier), `defineGlyph` for your own; `addLevelBodies`; the page registers the text (`createEngine({ levels })`); a building of storeys: `node x new level`; buildings on a terrain: `defineSite`, `addSiteBodies` | labs/game/scenes/play.ts, labs/box/levels/hall.txt, labs/playground/levels/site.ts |\n| A player or character          | `createCharacter` (speed, jump, dash, coyote time, buffer), `spawnActor`, `measureFeel`; Apex's movement, `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`, or your own: `defineMovement` (`node x new movement`); `node x feel`, `createFeelPanel` | labs/game/cast/player.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts, labs/playground/cast/player.ts |\n| Physics                        | `physics: true` in the scene, `physicsOf(w)`: bodies, `raycast`, `overlap`, `probe`; `addBox`, `addSensor`, `knock`, movers | labs/box/scenes/hall.ts |\n| Props                          | `defineProp` (meshes, a body, hooks), `spawnProp`, `hitProp`; the engine's `prop:crate`, `prop:barrel`, `prop:brazier`, `prop:pillar`; one a player uses or strikes: `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `addInteraction` (`node x new interactable`) | labs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts |\n| Rides, doors and switches      | `addLevelLadders`, `addZipline`, `addLauncher`; `addLevelDoors`, `addLevelWindows`, `addSwitch`, `switchGroup`; `setDaytime`, `lightsAtDusk`; `placeActor` | labs/playground/scenes/site.ts |\n| Enemies and crowds             | Crowd bodies (`addAgent`, `updateCrowd`, `knockback`, `killAgent`), steering (`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`), `NavGrid` and `FlowField`, attack tokens, `canSee` | labs/box/cast/walker.ts, labs/box/scenes/crowd-1000.ts |\n| Combat                         | Moves as data (`defineMove`, `Attack`, `Combo`), hit queries (`coneHits`, `sweepHits`), `damage` and `HEALTH`, projectiles, telegraphs, `reactTo`, `requestHitStop` | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts |\n| Melee, guns and targets        | `addMelee` (punches, kicks, the door kick), `defineWeapon`, `weaponsFor` and `addWeapons` (hip fire, the sights, a climbing recoil, reloads; `node x new weapon`), `defineThing` and `spawnThing` (targets that fall at a hit and stand again; `node x new thing`) | labs/playground/weapons/, labs/playground/cast/dummies.ts |\n| Ragdolls and joints            | `addJoint` (fixed, ball, hinge, slider, rope, spring, as data), `addRagdolls` and `handOff` (a hard blow or a death gives a person's body to physics; it gets up the way it lies), `standUp` | labs/box/scenes/ragdoll.ts, labs/playground/cast/partners.ts |\n| Animation                      | Actors animate themselves (`spawnActor({ anim: { build } })`); builds, bodies, the animator's body states, actions (`defineAction`, played on an actor with `actOn`), clips from the library (`playClip`) | labs/box/scenes/clips.ts, `node x anim find` |\n| How it looks                   | Materials and textures as data (`defineMaterial`, `defineTexture`), looks (`?post.look=look:storybook`, `post.combine`, `post.filters`, `defineLook`; `__engine.look()`), skies, lights | labs/box/looks/torch-lit.ts, `node x describe look` |\n| Effects                        | Emitters as data, bursting on sim events (`defineEmitter`); telegraphs; screen shake      | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts           |\n| UI                             | `engine.ui`: `hud(id, text, { at })`, `label`, `damage`, `crosshair`; screens and menus: `createMenu`, `createEngine({ store, modal })` (rebinds kept, a menu's keys out of play), `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen` (a controls screen); the rest plain HTML/CSS | labs/game/main.ts (its pause), labs/playground/ui/, labs/box/menu.ts |\n| Sound                          | Effects and songs as data (`defineSfx`, `defineSong`), the page's `createAudio`; a scene's `sounds` maps events to sounds | labs/box/sounds/effects.ts, labs/box/sounds/page.ts |\n| Cameras                        | `?cam=iso` (`threequarter`, `topdown`, `overhead`, `brawler`, `side`), `?cam3=chase` (`orbit`, `fly`, `fixed`, `first`, `rail`, `shoulder`, `follow`); `__engine.camera.set(code, { blend: true })`; a map per mode: `createEngine({ actionMap: (code) => … })`; a mode of your own: `defineCamera` (`node x new camera`) | labs/game/main.ts, labs/playground/views.ts |\n| Touch controls                 | `defineTouchLayout`, `createEngine({ touchLayout })` (built, not yet tested on phones)   | labs/box/touch.ts                  |\n| A new entry of a kind          | `node x new <kind> labs/game/<dir>/<name>`: the module and its test from a template (`node x new` lists the kinds, tools/templates/README.md says where each goes; a new scene starts as a copy of play.ts instead) | tools/templates/                    |\n\nSaves, high scores and settings go in the page's store (`createStore`, never read by the sim), as labs/game/best.ts\nkeeps the best round.\n\n## 5. Rules that keep play reproducible\n\n- Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts; pages only engine/index.ts.\n  Never import three.js or Rapier directly: the public API re-exports what a game needs.\n- No clocks, timers, `Math.random`, `crypto`, DOM, storage, Web Audio or Node built-ins in sim-side code, and no\n  dynamic `import()`: lint refuses each, and its message names the fix. No network either: what the sim needs from\n  outside arrives as intents or settings. Write `Math.pow`, not `**`. Use the standard `Math`: the sim swaps in\n  portable ports while it steps, so the results match in Node and Chromium.\n- Time in steps; randomness from named streams; entities in `w.query`'s id order.\n- A sim-side module reads no files: a level's text lives in a string in the scene module.\n- Presentation never writes the sim: cameras, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change freely without changing a hash.\n- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n\n**Assets.** Make them with code and data first: geometry, textures and materials as generators with parameters,\nsounds as voices and tracker notes, levels as text. A binary file (an image, a recorded sound, a model) needs the\nowner's approval: ask, and once they approve, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json at the game's root as\n`{ \"path\", \"reason\" }`, the reason their answer, who and when; `npm run check` fails a binary listed nowhere. Fonts\nare approved: anywhere in labs/game/, each with its license file beside it.\n\n## 6. Seeing it without a screen\n\nEvery command prints its verdict first, about 20 lines at most, and writes `out/<cmd>/<target>/report.json`;\n`node x help <cmd>` gives its manual.\n\n| Command                                                     | What it does                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `npm test`                                                  | The game's tests (Vitest), headless                                              |\n| `npm run check`                                             | Types, lint, format, `node x check` (the asset rules; the engine as pinned)       |\n| `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--steps n] [--seed s] [--set k=v] [--feel]` | Runs a scene in Node: its hash, events, entities; `--feel` measures the player |\n| `node x replay labs/game/replays [--browser sim] [--update] [--bisect]` | Checks replays' goldens; `--update` records them; `--bisect` finds where two runs part |\n| `node x shot labs/game [--cam code] [--set k=v] [--png]`    | Renders the page on WebGPU: coverage, visible objects, a text thumbnail; an image only with `--png` |\n| `node x eye labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--cam code] [--steps n]` | What a camera sees of the bodies, in Node with no GPU: a text picture and its legend (`__engine.eye()` in a page) |\n| `node x film labs/game --steps \"…\" [--compare ref]`         | Films scripted play as a contact sheet; `--compare` names the objects whose pixels changed |\n| `node x gallery <kind> [id] --page labs/game [--look id]`   | Films every prop, material, emitter, look, body or move as a labelled contact sheet; `#gallery/<kind>/<id>` opens one on any page |\n| `node x inspect --page labs/game`, then `node x eval \"<js>\"`, `node x dump`, `node x set <key> <value>` | A live headless page to question: the HUD through DOM queries, `__engine` |\n| `node x perf labs/game [--scene id]`                        | Performance as counters (ms per step and frame, draw calls), never frames per second |\n| `node x describe [kind [id]]`                               | The registries: every kind's fields and entries (materials, moves, looks, sounds…) |\n| `node x qa geo tex audio`                                   | Content QA by numbers: the engine's and the game's geometry, props, textures and sounds (labs/game/'s geometry, props, textures and sounds folders); a new or worse violation fails, and one the game accepts goes in its own `tests/baselines/qa-<family>-game.json` with the reason |\n| `node x new <kind> <id>`                                    | A new entry from its template, with its test                                     |\n| `node x audio sfx:<id>` · `node x anim find <words>`        | A sound rendered in Node, as numbers · the clip library searched                 |\n| `node x engine`                                             | Which engine the game is on, and whether its files are as shipped                |\n\nIn a page, `window.__engine` is the inspector, and a headless engine in tests (`createHeadless({ scene, seed,\nsettings })`) has the same members but the rendering ones: `__engine.help()` lists them (`step`, `hash`,\n`capture`, `restore`, `replay`, `input.press`, `input.record`, `set`, `state`, `shot`,\n`stats`…).\n\n## 7. Testing a game\n\n- The starter's test shows the four kinds every game keeps: its golden replay, a run that continues exactly after a\n  capture and a restore, the bot winning on 10 seeds, and a rule (the clock). Tests sit beside their module\n  (`play.test.ts`) and run with `npm test`.\n- Keep the bot as the game grows: teach it each new rule. \"The bot wins on 10 seeds\" keeps proving the game can be won,\n  and finds the seeds that softlock.\n- Check the UI through the DOM: the overlay's elements are `[data-ui]` (`node x inspect`, then `node x eval`).\n- When play changes on purpose, record the goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) and\n  say why in the commit. When a hash changes by surprise, `node x replay <file> --browser sim --bisect` narrows it\n  down: to the step, entity and field where two runs part (Node and Chromium, or Node and Node again), or, when every\n  run agrees but they all left the goldens, to the checkpoints between which they did (then the code, data or a\n  dependency changed what the sim does). The usual causes: a `Math` function held in a variable, `**`, an iteration\n  whose order is not fixed, module state between runs, view settings or presentation reaching the sim, `async` code in\n  a step.\n\n## 8. Looking things up\n\n- **docs/API.md**: every public name with the first sentence of its doc comment, the public API first. The comment at\n  the link is the full manual: grep engine/ for the name.\n- **docs/ERRORS.md**: every error and advice code with its fix. Errors print as `[CODE] message`, and the fix says\n  what to do.\n- **The engine's file comments**: each module opens with what it is for, its rules and a runnable `@example`.\n- **`node x describe`** for data, **`node x help`** for commands, **`__engine.help()`** for the inspector.\n- **The engine's repository** (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge) for its plan, its decisions and its tests.\n\n## 9. When the engine is in your way\n\n1. Work round it in the game: a component, a system, page code of your own.\n2. Write it in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md for an engine agent who has never seen your game: what you tried, what happened\n   (the command, the error code), what you expected, what you did instead. The owner collects these from every game.\n3. Never change the engine quietly: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json. When no\n   workaround exists, patch the file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, with why.\n4. The owner decides when a game moves to a newer engine (`node x engine --update`). It replaces nothing of the\n   game's own (it stops and names what is in the way); the engine's package.json entries win, and it names any it\n   drops or sets back. It says which root files it brought to the engine's templates, and which ones you edited\n   whose templates changed: compare those with tools/game/template/ and take what applies. After it: `npm ci`,\n   `npm run check`, `npm test`, and record the goldens again where play changed.\n\n## 10. The public API by area\n\n<!-- api-map:start: generated by `node x docs --write` from the area comments of engine/sim-api.ts, engine/anim/api.ts and engine/index.ts; edit those, not this section -->\nSim-side code imports these from engine/sim-api.ts; pages get them from engine/index.ts as well. Each name has the\nfirst sentence of its doc comment in docs/API.md, and its whole manual where it is declared.\n\n**Scenes**: defined as data with a setup and a step, run by x sim, x replay and createHeadless.\n`defineScene`, `defineDevAction`; types `Scene`, `SceneSpec`, `SceneAction`, `SceneSound`, `DevActionSpec`.\n\n**The world**: entities, components, systems, timers, events.\n`defineComponent`, `PHASES`; types `World`, `SimRng`, `WorldTimers`, `WorldEvents`, `Entity`, `With`, `SpawnSpec`, `ComponentOf`, `Phase`, `SystemFn`.\n\n**Registries and settings**: content as data, one schema for every setting.\n`defineKind`, `def`, `defineSettings`; types `Entry`, `Kind`, `KindSpec`, `Field`, `Schema`, `SimSettings`, `SettingValue`.\n\n**Intents**: what the sim reads from outside each step.\n`held`, `pressed`, `fromCamera`, `INTENT_KEYS`, `createInputBuffer`, `updateInputBuffer`, `released`, `buffered`, `consume`, `consumeAll`, `repeated`, `BUFFER_STEPS`, `REPEAT_DELAY`, `REPEAT_RATE`; types `Intents`, `IntentValue`, `CustomIntentKey`, `InputBuffer`.\n\n**Math**: three.js's math classes and the engine's helpers (MathUtils covers clamp, lerp, damp, smoothstep).\n`Vector3`, `Quaternion`, `Matrix4`, `Euler`, `Box3`, `Sphere`, `Ray`, `Plane`, `Color`, `MathUtils`, `approach`, `angDiff`, `lerpAng`, `approachAng`, `smoothDamp`, `ease`, `swingTwist`.\n\n**Randomness**: seeded streams (the world's are w.rng('<stream>')).\n`Rng`.\n\n**Events**: emitted with w.emit, heard with w.events.on.\ntypes `EventMap`, `Listener`, `TraceRecord`.\n\n**Levels**: a legend of glyphs, level text compiled to plain data (heights, solid grid, colliders, meshes, spawns).\n`compileLevel`, `validateLevel`, `defineGlyph`, `defineLevel`, `getLevel`, `floorHeight`, `topHeight`, `solidAt`, `tileAt`, `LEVEL_FORMAT`; types `CompileOptions`, `LevelSource`, `LevelProblem`, `Glyph`, `GlyphSpec`, `LevelEntry`, `CompiledLevel`, `LevelGlyph`, `BoxCollider`, `ColliderDescriptor`, `GroundDescriptor`, `MeshDescriptor`, `SpawnPoint`, `LightSpot`, `TileRect`, `Point3`.\n\n**Animation (WP 3.4)**: the humanoid skeleton (a root plus 23 joints) and its builds, poses with three.js quaternions, blending under bone masks, additive layers, forward kinematics, SVG contact sheets and the pose checks.\n`JOINTS`, `SOCKETS`, `MASKS`, `createSkeleton`, `boneMask`, `defineBuild`, `getBuild`, `Pose`, `blendPoses`, `makeAdditive`, `addPose`, `hashPose`, `forwardKinematics`, `createWorldPose`, `jointPosition`, `jointRotation`, `jointMatrix`, `jointToWorld`, `socketPosition`, `tipPosition`, `animSheet`, `animCheck`, `twoBone`, `twoBoneRot`, `LIMBS`, `aim`, `AIM_CHAIN`, `footPlant`, `createFootPlantState`, `FOOT_LOCK`, `FOOT_PLANT`, `FootPlant`, `queryGround`, `handTo`; types `JointName`, `SocketName`, `MaskName`, `Skeleton`, `BoneMask`, `Build`, `BuildSpec`, `JointRef`, `BlendOptions`, `WorldPose`, `Sheet`, `SheetFrame`, `SheetOptions`, `SheetView`, `AnimCheckSpec`, `AnimCheckReport`, `AnimProblem`, `RigidPair`, `PointName`, `TwoBoneOptions`, `LimbEnd`, `Limb`, `AimOptions`, `AimLink`, `FootPlantOptions`, `FootPlantState`, `FootLock`, `FootPlantLayerOptions`, `GroundQueries`, `GroundProbe`, `GroundHit`.\n\n**The procedural humanoid (WP 3.6)**: my-3d2dge's gait, idle, lean, arm swing, held poses and stances as effector targets written with B(forward, right, up), solved onto the skeleton; a fixed step and a seeded stream.\n`Humanoid`, `POSES`, `STANCES`, `WEAPONS`, `B`, `turnRight`; types `HumanoidOptions`, `HumanoidInput`, `HumanoidState`, `HumanoidWeights`, `BodyMotion`, `PoseName`, `StanceName`, `Weapon`, `Effectors`, `FRU`, `LimbSide`, `Sided`.\n\n**The character animator (WP 3.7)**: a body driven through an explicit layer stack (weights, base, override, solve, then layers in clips, additive, post, physics and secondary), told its body states each step (unknown words warn with the closest), with footsteps, sockets, a trail and detail levels; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n`Animator`, `BODY_STATES`, `checkBodyStates`, `TRAILS`, `SAMPLE_STATES`, `SLOTS`, `LAYER_SLOTS`, `LOD`, `easeWeight`, `HUMANOID_WEIGHTS`; types `AnimatorOptions`, `PoseOptions`, `AnimatorReport`, `AnimEvent`, `TrailSample`, `BodySt","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-024328--x54facc-work/9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae/tool-results/bsy4z2ug0.txt","persistedOutputSize":92894},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PNffXJDQEFLrNx1dDAXZmh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjZjzm3HjaF7tnbYT2nz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012biEMd1vkMnmCWS2v61TfW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-024328--x54facc-work/9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae/tool-results/bsy4z2ug0.txt","allow_large":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1108,"cache_read_input_tokens":64434,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1108},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae","uuid":"98c29097-69c7-41b1-9f31-6039cc3dbb6c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:44:14.083Z","request_id":"req_011CgjZjzWfDC388vM7QZsbj","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012biEMd1vkMnmCWS2v61TfW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-024328--x54facc-work/9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae/tool-results/bsy4z2ug0.txt","allow_large":true}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012biEMd1vkMnmCWS2v61TfW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md: weetare\n2\t\n3\tweetare is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\n4\tWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\n5\tis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n6\t\n7\tBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\n8\tit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\n9\tthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n10\t\n11\t## Set up (once per machine)\n12\t\n13\t    bash scripts/setup.sh\n14\t\n15\tIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n16\t`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n17\t\n18\t## The loop\n19\t\n20\t1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n21\t   labs/game/main.ts.\n22\t2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n23\t3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n24\t4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n25\t   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n26\t   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n27\t   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n28\t5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n29\t\n30\t`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n31\t\n32\t## Rules\n33\t\n34\t- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n35\t  configs and the docs it shipped: engine-lock.json lists every one, and `npm run check` fails when one changes. When\n36\t  the engine is in your way, work round it in the game and write what you needed in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. Only when no\n37\t  workaround exists, patch the engine file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" there, with why.\n38\t- **Only the public API.** Pages import engine/index.ts; sim-side code imports engine/sim-api.ts (lint enforces it).\n39\t- **Reproducible play.** Sim-side code reads no clock, no `Math.random`, no DOM: randomness comes from named streams\n40\t  (`w.rng('loot')`), time is whole 60 Hz steps. The same intents and seed give the same hashes, in Node and Chromium.\n41\t- **Assets are code or text first**: geometry, textures, materials and sounds as data. A binary file only with the\n42\t  owner's approval, listed in APPROVED-BINARIES.json with their answer (`npm run check` fails one unlisted); fonts are\n43\t  approved, anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them.\n44\t- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines.\n45\t- **Ask the owner** before anything outward or costly: a binary asset, a new npm dependency, spending money,\n46\t  publishing. No answer within 15 minutes: take the safer choice, note it in labs/game/README.md, carry on.\n47\t- Update the engine only when the owner asks: `node x engine --update`.\n48\t\n49\t## Done means\n50\t\n51\t`npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the bot still wins (teach it each new rule); a change that alters play on\n52\tpurpose records the replay's goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) with the reason\n53\tin the commit; labs/game/README.md says what the game is now.\n54\t# Making a game with my-3dge\n55\t\n56\tYou are making a game with the my-3dge engine. Read this once, start to finish; after that, look things up (section\n57\t8). A game's AGENTS.md sends you here after `node tools/game/new-game.mjs` made the game. For a model without the\n58\trepository, this one file is also the engine in brief: its public API by area is section 10.\n59\t\n60\t## 1. What you have\n61\t\n62\tmy-3dge is a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier physics, the\n63\tUI in HTML/CSS. Three promises shape everything you write:\n64\t\n65\t- **Play is reproducible.** The rules (the _sim_) run on the CPU at a fixed 60 steps a second. The same seed and the\n66\t  same intents give the same state, bit for bit, in Node and in Chromium; every state has a 16-digit hash.\n67\t- **Nothing needs a screen.** The sim runs headless in Node (tests, `node x sim`). The page renders on WebGPU in a\n68\t  headless Chromium (`node x shot`) and reports what it drew as numbers: which object covers which pixels (the ID\n69\t  pass), the look's metrics, a text thumbnail. You prove a game works with commands, not by looking.\n70\t- **Code and data, not binary files.** Levels are text maps; materials and textures are generators with parameters;\n71\t  sounds are voices and tracker notes; characters are procedural bodies, with a library of readable motion clips.\n72\t\n73\tA game's repository:\n74\t\n75\t| Path                                                             | What it is                                                            |\n76\t| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n77\t| `labs/game/`                                                     | The game: the page, the rules (`scenes/`), the characters (`cast/`), the bot, the replays |\n78\t| `labs/box/`                                                      | The engine's worked example: a hero, crowds, combat, props, looks, sound (read-only)   |\n79\t| `engine/`                                                        | The engine. Game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts            |\n80\t| `tools/`, `x.js`                                                 | The `node x` commands, the lint rules, the templates (tools/templates/)               |\n81\t| `data/anim/`                                                     | The animation library: readable clips, and the engine's actions                       |\n82\t| `docs/`                                                          | This guide, API.md (every public name) and ERRORS.md (every error code, with its fix) |\n83\t| `engine-lock.json`, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`                         | The engine's pinned commit and file hashes; what you needed from the engine           |\n84\t\n85\tEverything but `labs/game/` and the root's game files (AGENTS.md, README.md, package.json…) is the engine's,\n86\tread-only: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json.\n87\t\n88\t## 2. The model\n89\t\n90\t**Two halves.** The sim is the game's rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them each\n91\tstep, events. Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts and never touches the\n92\tDOM, the GPU, the clock or `Math.random`. The page (labs/game/main.ts) imports engine/index.ts: `createEngine` starts\n93\tthe sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into intents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame`\n94\tfor the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The page reads the sim and never writes it. Lint enforces the split.\n95\t\n96\t**A scene** is the unit `x sim`, the page and the tests run: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings,\n97\tsetup(w), step(w, intents) })`. `setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. One page can\n98\toffer several scenes (`createEngine({ scenes })`, `?scene=<id>` picks one); a running page cannot switch scenes yet,\n99\tso a level select reloads the page with another `?scene`.\n100\t\n101\t**The world, `w`**, is what systems get:\n102\t\n103\t- **Components and entities.** `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields\n104\t  with defaults and units, so the state can be hashed, captured and checked. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an\n105\t  id; `w.query('gem')` returns the live entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)`, `w.add`, `w.remove` and\n106\t  `w.despawn` do the rest. An entity whose component declares `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box until\n107\t  something draws it properly (an actor, a prop, a crowd body).\n108\t- **Systems** run in phases, in this order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`.\n109\t  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` puts a rule after the physics moved the bodies and the\n110\t  readback copied their places into components.\n111\t- **Events.** `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)` listeners. A page\n112\t  reads them from `frame.world.events.trace()` (sounds, effects); emitters burst on them; tests count them.\n113\t- **Time** is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800), never by\n114\t  adding up `w.dt`: floating-point sums drift. `w.timers` schedule in sim seconds.\n115\t- **Randomness** comes from named streams seeded by the run: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n116\t- **Settings** are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum,\n117\t  unit, when: 'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`. Each is reachable from the URL\n118\t  (`?game.gems=20`), from `node x sim … --set game.gems=20`, `node x set` and `__engine.set`, and replays record them.\n119\t\n120\t**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once per step, as plain data: `move` (a world-space\n121\tdirection `[x, z]`), `b` (buttons held), `p` (pressed this step), `aim`, `look`, `cam`, and the game's own\n122\t`'game:<name>'` keys. A player actor reads the step's intents itself; systems get them as their second argument\n123\t(`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). In a page, devices make intents through an action map\n124\t(`actionMap:thirdPerson` by default: WASD or the arrows run, Space jumps, Shift dashes); a map with `look: true` sends\n125\tthe camera's view as `look`, and its `aimWith` fills `aim` (`centre`: what the screen's middle points at; `pointer`:\n126\tthe pointer on the player's floor), and a game with several camera modes gives `createEngine` a map per camera\n127\t(`actionMap: (code) => …`). A bot or a test writes them directly: `engine.step(1, { move: [1, 0] })`. A replay is the\n128\tintents of a run, recorded.\n129\t\n130\t**Proof.** `engine.hash()` digests the whole sim. A replay file (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json) holds a scene,\n131\tseed, settings, the intents at each step that changed them, and the golden hashes at its checkpoints, per platform\n132\t(`linux-x64`). `capture()` and `restore()` copy a run and continue it exactly. If a hash changes, play changed.\n133\t\n134\t## 3. The starter, file by file\n135\t\n136\tThe starter (labs/game/) is a whole small game: run round an arena collecting every gem before the clock runs out.\n137\tGrow your game from it; keep its tests as they are, and change what they check as the game changes.\n138\t\n139\t| File                         | What it shows                                                                              |\n140\t| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n141\t| labs/game/scenes/play.ts     | The rules: a level as text, settings, components (`gem`, `round`), systems, a random stream, events |\n142\t| labs/game/cast/player.ts     | The player: a character on the controller (run, jump, dash) drawn as the humanoid          |\n143\t| labs/game/bot.ts             | A bot that plays through intents, steering down a flow field to the nearest gem            |\n144\t| labs/game/main.ts            | The page: `createEngine`, the HUD in `onFrame`, Enter to play again, Esc's pause menu, the chase camera     |\n145\t| labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts | The tests: the golden replay, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds, the clock     |\n146\t| labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json | The bot's play, recorded, with its golden hashes                                      |\n147\t| labs/game/README.md          | The game's own page: what it is, how it plays, the calls made                              |\n148\t\n149\t## 4. What to reach for\n150\t\n151\tThe public names below are in engine/sim-api.ts (sim-side) or engine/index.ts (pages); section 10 lists them all.\n152\tThe box (labs/box/, its README says which scene shows what) has a working use of almost each one.\n153\t\n154\t| You need                       | Reach for                                                                                 | See it in                          |\n155\t| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n156\t| A level                        | A text map in a scene module; `compileLevel`, glyphs (`#` wall, `.` floor, `@` spawn, `w` low wall, `P` pillar, `^` stairs, `=` gallery, `o` dais, `t` brazier), `defineGlyph` for your own; `addLevelBodies`; the page registers the text (`createEngine({ levels })`); a building of storeys: `node x new level`; buildings on a terrain: `defineSite`, `addSiteBodies` | labs/game/scenes/play.ts, labs/box/levels/hall.txt, labs/playground/levels/site.ts |\n157\t| A player or character          | `createCharacter` (speed, jump, dash, coyote time, buffer), `spawnActor`, `measureFeel`; Apex's movement, `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`, or your own: `defineMovement` (`node x new movement`); `node x feel`, `createFeelPanel` | labs/game/cast/player.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts, labs/playground/cast/player.ts |\n158\t| Physics                        | `physics: true` in the scene, `physicsOf(w)`: bodies, `raycast`, `overlap`, `probe`; `addBox`, `addSensor`, `knock`, movers | labs/box/scenes/hall.ts |\n159\t| Props                          | `defineProp` (meshes, a body, hooks), `spawnProp`, `hitProp`; the engine's `prop:crate`, `prop:barrel`, `prop:brazier`, `prop:pillar`; one a player uses or strikes: `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `addInteraction` (`node x new interactable`) | labs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts |\n160\t| Rides, doors and switches      | `addLevelLadders`, `addZipline`, `addLauncher`; `addLevelDoors`, `addLevelWindows`, `addSwitch`, `switchGroup`; `setDaytime`, `lightsAtDusk`; `placeActor` | labs/playground/scenes/site.ts |\n161\t| Enemies and crowds             | Crowd bodies (`addAgent`, `updateCrowd`, `knockback`, `killAgent`), steering (`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`), `NavGrid` and `FlowField`, attack tokens, `canSee` | labs/box/cast/walker.ts, labs/box/scenes/crowd-1000.ts |\n162\t| Combat                         | Moves as data (`defineMove`, `Attack`, `Combo`), hit queries (`coneHits`, `sweepHits`), `damage` and `HEALTH`, projectiles, telegraphs, `reactTo`, `requestHitStop` | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts |\n163\t| Melee, guns and targets        | `addMelee` (punches, kicks, the door kick), `defineWeapon`, `weaponsFor` and `addWeapons` (hip fire, the sights, a climbing recoil, reloads; `node x new weapon`), `defineThing` and `spawnThing` (targets that fall at a hit and stand again; `node x new thing`) | labs/playground/weapons/, labs/playground/cast/dummies.ts |\n164\t| Ragdolls and joints            | `addJoint` (fixed, ball, hinge, slider, rope, spring, as data), `addRagdolls` and `handOff` (a hard blow or a death gives a person's body to physics; it gets up the way it lies), `standUp` | labs/box/scenes/ragdoll.ts, labs/playground/cast/partners.ts |\n165\t| Animation                      | Actors animate themselves (`spawnActor({ anim: { build } })`); builds, bodies, the animator's body states, actions (`defineAction`, played on an actor with `actOn`), clips from the library (`playClip`) | labs/box/scenes/clips.ts, `node x anim find` |\n166\t| How it looks                   | Materials and textures as data (`defineMaterial`, `defineTexture`), looks (`?post.look=look:storybook`, `post.combine`, `post.filters`, `defineLook`; `__engine.look()`), skies, lights | labs/box/looks/torch-lit.ts, `node x describe look` |\n167\t| Effects                        | Emitters as data, bursting on sim events (`defineEmitter`); telegraphs; screen shake      | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts           |\n168\t| UI                             | `engine.ui`: `hud(id, text, { at })`, `label`, `damage`, `crosshair`; screens and menus: `createMenu`, `createEngine({ store, modal })` (rebinds kept, a menu's keys out of play), `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen` (a controls screen); the rest plain HTML/CSS | labs/game/main.ts (its pause), labs/playground/ui/, labs/box/menu.ts |\n169\t| Sound                          | Effects and songs as data (`defineSfx`, `defineSong`), the page's `createAudio`; a scene's `sounds` maps events to sounds | labs/box/sounds/effects.ts, labs/box/sounds/page.ts |\n170\t| Cameras                        | `?cam=iso` (`threequarter`, `topdown`, `overhead`, `brawler`, `side`), `?cam3=chase` (`orbit`, `fly`, `fixed`, `first`, `rail`, `shoulder`, `follow`); `__engine.camera.set(code, { blend: true })`; a map per mode: `createEngine({ actionMap: (code) => … })`; a mode of your own: `defineCamera` (`node x new camera`) | labs/game/main.ts, labs/playground/views.ts |\n171\t| Touch controls                 | `defineTouchLayout`, `createEngine({ touchLayout })` (built, not yet tested on phones)   | labs/box/touch.ts                  |\n172\t| A new entry of a kind          | `node x new <kind> labs/game/<dir>/<name>`: the module and its test from a template (`node x new` lists the kinds, tools/templates/README.md says where each goes; a new scene starts as a copy of play.ts instead) | tools/templates/                    |\n173\t\n174\tSaves, high scores and settings go in the page's store (`createStore`, never read by the sim), as labs/game/best.ts\n175\tkeeps the best round.\n176\t\n177\t## 5. Rules that keep play reproducible\n178\t\n179\t- Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts; pages only engine/index.ts.\n180\t  Never import three.js or Rapier directly: the public API re-exports what a game needs.\n181\t- No clocks, timers, `Math.random`, `crypto`, DOM, storage, Web Audio or Node built-ins in sim-side code, and no\n182\t  dynamic `import()`: lint refuses each, and its message names the fix. No network either: what the sim needs from\n183\t  outside arrives as intents or settings. Write `Math.pow`, not `**`. Use the standard `Math`: the sim swaps in\n184\t  portable ports while it steps, so the results match in Node and Chromium.\n185\t- Time in steps; randomness from named streams; entities in `w.query`'s id order.\n186\t- A sim-side module reads no files: a level's text lives in a string in the scene module.\n187\t- Presentation never writes the sim: cameras, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change freely without changing a hash.\n188\t- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n189\t\n190\t**Assets.** Make them with code and data first: geometry, textures and materials as generators with parameters,\n191\tsounds as voices and tracker notes, levels as text. A binary file (an image, a recorded sound, a model) needs the\n192\towner's approval: ask, and once they approve, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json at the game's root as\n193\t`{ \"path\", \"reason\" }`, the reason their answer, who and when; `npm run check` fails a binary listed nowhere. Fonts\n194\tare approved: anywhere in labs/game/, each with its license file beside it.\n195\t\n196\t## 6. Seeing it without a screen\n197\t\n198\tEvery command prints its verdict first, about 20 lines at most, and writes `out/<cmd>/<target>/report.json`;\n199\t`node x help <cmd>` gives its manual.\n200\t\n201\t| Command                                                     | What it does                                                                     |\n202\t| ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n203\t| `npm test`                                                  | The game's tests (Vitest), headless                                              |\n204\t| `npm run check`                                             | Types, lint, format, `node x check` (the asset rules; the engine as pinned)       |\n205\t| `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--steps n] [--seed s] [--set k=v] [--feel]` | Runs a scene in Node: its hash, events, entities; `--feel` measures the player |\n206\t| `node x replay labs/game/replays [--browser sim] [--update] [--bisect]` | Checks replays' goldens; `--update` records them; `--bisect` finds where two runs part |\n207\t| `node x shot labs/game [--cam code] [--set k=v] [--png]`    | Renders the page on WebGPU: coverage, visible objects, a text thumbnail; an image only with `--png` |\n208\t| `node x eye labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--cam code] [--steps n]` | What a camera sees of the bodies, in Node with no GPU: a text picture and its legend (`__engine.eye()` in a page) |\n209\t| `node x film labs/game --steps \"…\" [--compare ref]`         | Films scripted play as a contact sheet; `--compare` names the objects whose pixels changed |\n210\t| `node x gallery <kind> [id] --page labs/game [--look id]`   | Films every prop, material, emitter, look, body or move as a labelled contact sheet; `#gallery/<kind>/<id>` opens one on any page |\n211\t| `node x inspect --page labs/game`, then `node x eval \"<js>\"`, `node x dump`, `node x set <key> <value>` | A live headless page to question: the HUD through DOM queries, `__engine` |\n212\t| `node x perf labs/game [--scene id]`                        | Performance as counters (ms per step and frame, draw calls), never frames per second |\n213\t| `node x describe [kind [id]]`                               | The registries: every kind's fields and entries (materials, moves, looks, sounds…) |\n214\t| `node x qa geo tex audio`                                   | Content QA by numbers: the engine's and the game's geometry, props, textures and sounds (labs/game/'s geometry, props, textures and sounds folders); a new or worse violation fails, and one the game accepts goes in its own `tests/baselines/qa-<family>-game.json` with the reason |\n215\t| `node x new <kind> <id>`                                    | A new entry from its template, with its test                                     |\n216\t| `node x audio sfx:<id>` · `node x anim find <words>`        | A sound rendered in Node, as numbers · the clip library searched                 |\n217\t| `node x engine`                                             | Which engine the game is on, and whether its files are as shipped                |\n218\t\n219\tIn a page, `window.__engine` is the inspector, and a headless engine in tests (`createHeadless({ scene, seed,\n220\tsettings })`) has the same members but the rendering ones: `__engine.help()` lists them (`step`, `hash`,\n221\t`capture`, `restore`, `replay`, `input.press`, `input.record`, `set`, `state`, `shot`,\n222\t`stats`…).\n223\t\n224\t## 7. Testing a game\n225\t\n226\t- The starter's test shows the four kinds every game keeps: its golden replay, a run that continues exactly after a\n227\t  capture and a restore, the bot winning on 10 seeds, and a rule (the clock). Tests sit beside their module\n228\t  (`play.test.ts`) and run with `npm test`.\n229\t- Keep the bot as the game grows: teach it each new rule. \"The bot wins on 10 seeds\" keeps proving the game can be won,\n230\t  and finds the seeds that softlock.\n231\t- Check the UI through the DOM: the overlay's elements are `[data-ui]` (`node x inspect`, then `node x eval`).\n232\t- When play changes on purpose, record the goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) and\n233\t  say why in the commit. When a hash changes by surprise, `node x replay <file> --browser sim --bisect` narrows it\n234\t  down: to the step, entity and field where two runs part (Node and Chromium, or Node and Node again), or, when every\n235\t  run agrees but they all left the goldens, to the checkpoints between which they did (then the code, data or a\n236\t  dependency changed what the sim does). The usual causes: a `Math` function held in a variable, `**`, an iteration\n237\t  whose order is not fixed, module state between runs, view settings or presentation reaching the sim, `async` code in\n238\t  a step.\n239\t\n240\t## 8. Looking things up\n241\t\n242\t- **docs/API.md**: every public name with the first sentence of its doc comment, the public API first. The comment at\n243\t  the link is the full manual: grep engine/ for the name.\n244\t- **docs/ERRORS.md**: every error and advice code with its fix. Errors print as `[CODE] message`, and the fix says\n245\t  what to do.\n246\t- **The engine's file comments**: each module opens with what it is for, its rules and a runnable `@example`.\n247\t- **`node x describe`** for data, **`node x help`** for commands, **`__engine.help()`** for the inspector.\n248\t- **The engine's repository** (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge) for its plan, its decisions and its tests.\n249\t\n250\t## 9. When the engine is in your way\n251\t\n252\t1. Work round it in the game: a component, a system, page code of your own.\n253\t2. Write it in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md for an engine agent who has never seen your game: what you tried, what happened\n254\t   (the command, the error code), what you expected, what you did instead. The owner collects these from every game.\n255\t3. Never change the engine quietly: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json. When no\n256\t   workaround exists, patch the file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, with why.\n257\t4. The owner decides when a game moves to a newer engine (`node x engine --update`). It replaces nothing of the\n258\t   game's own (it stops and names what is in the way); the engine's package.json entries win, and it names any it\n259\t   drops or sets back. It says which root files it brought to the engine's templates, and which ones you edited\n260\t   whose templates changed: compare those with tools/game/template/ and take what applies. After it: `npm ci`,\n261\t   `npm run check`, `npm test`, and record the goldens again where play changed.\n262\t\n263\t## 10. The public API by area\n264\t\n265\t<!-- api-map:start: generated by `node x docs --write` from the area comments of engine/sim-api.ts, engine/anim/api.ts and engine/index.ts; edit those, not this section -->\n266\tSim-side code imports these from engine/sim-api.ts; pages get them from engine/index.ts as well. Each name has the\n267\tfirst sentence of its doc comment in docs/API.md, and its whole manual where it is declared.\n268\t\n269\t**Scenes**: defined as data with a setup and a step, run by x sim, x replay and createHeadless.\n270\t`defineScene`, `defineDevAction`; types `Scene`, `SceneSpec`, `SceneAction`, `SceneSound`, `DevActionSpec`.\n271\t\n272\t**The world**: entities, components, systems, timers, events.\n273\t`defineComponent`, `PHASES`; types `World`, `SimRng`, `WorldTimers`, `WorldEvents`, `Entity`, `With`, `SpawnSpec`, `ComponentOf`, `Phase`, `SystemFn`.\n274\t\n275\t**Registries and settings**: content as data, one schema for every setting.\n276\t`defineKind`, `def`, `defineSettings`; types `Entry`, `Kind`, `KindSpec`, `Field`, `Schema`, `SimSettings`, `SettingValue`.\n277\t\n278\t**Intents**: what the sim reads from outside each step.\n279\t`held`, `pressed`, `fromCamera`, `INTENT_KEYS`, `createInputBuffer`, `updateInputBuffer`, `released`, `buffered`, `consume`, `consumeAll`, `repeated`, `BUFFER_STEPS`, `REPEAT_DELAY`, `REPEAT_RATE`; types `Intents`, `IntentValue`, `CustomIntentKey`, `InputBuffer`.\n280\t\n281\t**Math**: three.js's math classes and the engine's helpers (MathUtils covers clamp, lerp, damp, smoothstep).\n282\t`Vector3`, `Quaternion`, `Matrix4`, `Euler`, `Box3`, `Sphere`, `Ray`, `Plane`, `Color`, `MathUtils`, `approach`, `angDiff`, `lerpAng`, `approachAng`, `smoothDamp`, `ease`, `swingTwist`.\n283\t\n284\t**Randomness**: seeded streams (the world's are w.rng('<stream>')).\n285\t`Rng`.\n286\t\n287\t**Events**: emitted with w.emit, heard with w.events.on.\n288\ttypes `EventMap`, `Listener`, `TraceRecord`.\n289\t\n290\t**Levels**: a legend of glyphs, level text compiled to plain data (heights, solid grid, colliders, meshes, spawns).\n291\t`compileLevel`, `validateLevel`, `defineGlyph`, `defineLevel`, `getLevel`, `floorHeight`, `topHeight`, `solidAt`, `tileAt`, `LEVEL_FORMAT`; types `CompileOptions`, `LevelSource`, `LevelProblem`, `Glyph`, `GlyphSpec`, `LevelEntry`, `CompiledLevel`, `LevelGlyph`, `BoxCollider`, `ColliderDescriptor`, `GroundDescriptor`, `MeshDescriptor`, `SpawnPoint`, `LightSpot`, `TileRect`, `Point3`.\n292\t\n293\t**Animation (WP 3.4)**: the humanoid skeleton (a root plus 23 joints) and its builds, poses with three.js quaternions, blending under bone masks, additive layers, forward kinematics, SVG contact sheets and the pose checks.\n294\t`JOINTS`, `SOCKETS`, `MASKS`, `createSkeleton`, `boneMask`, `defineBuild`, `getBuild`, `Pose`, `blendPoses`, `makeAdditive`, `addPose`, `hashPose`, `forwardKinematics`, `createWorldPose`, `jointPosition`, `jointRotation`, `jointMatrix`, `jointToWorld`, `socketPosition`, `tipPosition`, `animSheet`, `animCheck`, `twoBone`, `twoBoneRot`, `LIMBS`, `aim`, `AIM_CHAIN`, `footPlant`, `createFootPlantState`, `FOOT_LOCK`, `FOOT_PLANT`, `FootPlant`, `queryGround`, `handTo`; types `JointName`, `SocketName`, `MaskName`, `Skeleton`, `BoneMask`, `Build`, `BuildSpec`, `JointRef`, `BlendOptions`, `WorldPose`, `Sheet`, `SheetFrame`, `SheetOptions`, `SheetView`, `AnimCheckSpec`, `AnimCheckReport`, `AnimProblem`, `RigidPair`, `PointName`, `TwoBoneOptions`, `LimbEnd`, `Limb`, `AimOptions`, `AimLink`, `FootPlantOptions`, `FootPlantState`, `FootLock`, `FootPlantLayerOptions`, `GroundQueries`, `GroundProbe`, `GroundHit`.\n295\t\n296\t**The procedural humanoid (WP 3.6)**: my-3d2dge's gait, idle, lean, arm swing, held poses and stances as effector targets written with B(forward, right, up), solved onto the skeleton; a fixed step and a seeded stream.\n297\t`Humanoid`, `POSES`, `STANCES`, `WEAPONS`, `B`, `turnRight`; types `HumanoidOptions`, `HumanoidInput`, `HumanoidState`, `HumanoidWeights`, `BodyMotion`, `PoseName`, `StanceName`, `Weapon`, `Effectors`, `FRU`, `LimbSide`, `Sided`.\n298\t\n299\t**The character animator (WP 3.7)**: a body driven through an explicit layer stack (weights, base, override, solve, then layers in clips, additive, post, physics and secondary), told its body states each step (unknown words warn with the closest), with footsteps, sockets, a trail and detail levels; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n300\t`Animator`, `BODY_STATES`, `checkBodyStates`, `TRAILS`, `SAMPLE_STATES`, `SLOTS`, `LAYER_SLOTS`, `LOD`, `easeWeight`, `HUMANOID_WEIGHTS`; types `AnimatorOptions`, `PoseOptions`, `AnimatorReport`, `AnimEvent`, `TrailSample`, `BodyStateName`, `AnimatorInput`, `TrailName`, `SampleState`, `SlotName`, `LayerSlot`, `AnimLayer`, `WeightRates`, `WeightInfo`.\n301\t\n302\t**Blob and floater rigs (WP 5.3)**: my-3d2dge's squash-and-stretch blob on a minimal skeleton (a volume-preserving squash spring, a smoothed look, wings, its moves as data: bite, leap, pounce, spit) and a floater that hovers over the floor, banks into turns and carries orbiting motes; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n303\t`Blob`, `BLOB`, `BLOB_JOINTS`, `BLOB_LEAP`, `BLOB_MOVES`, `blobMove`, `blobMoveTimes`, `Floater`, `FLOATER`; types `BlobOptions`, `BlobInput`, `BlobState`, `BlobJoint`, `BlobPose`, `BlobLeap`, `BlobMove`, `BlobMoveName`, `BlobMoveFrame`, `FloaterOptions`, `FloaterInput`, `FloaterState`, `FloaterPose`.\n304\t\n305\t**The animation library (WP 8.4)**: readable key-pose clips (format 1 and 2: parse, print, check, the normalized lens, mirroring, the decode to 36 body points), loaded per set; baked to 30 fps rotation tracks on the skeleton, retargeted onto any build (rotation copy aligned at rest, the hip-height ratio, contact IK) or given to three.js as AnimationClip JSON; played by the clip layer in the animator's clip slot, loops at the ground speed, with shardfall's walk-run switch and strike time.\n306\t`parseClip`, `parseClipSet`, `validateClip`, `poseError`, `poseMeanError`, `clipText`, `normalizedLens`, `mirrorClip`, `decodePose`, `samplePose`, `POSE_POINTS`, `POINT_INDEX`, `bakeClip`, `sampleBaked`, `travelAt`, `BAKE_FPS`, `retargetPose`, `clipJSON`, `hipRatio`, `standingHipHeight`, `restSkeleton`, `restPoints`, `restBuild`, `RETARGET`, `loadClipLibrary`, `clipPace`, `walkRate`, `runOverWalk`, `strikeTime`, `GROUND_SPEED`, `STRIKE`, `ClipLayer`, `CLIP_LAYER`; types `ClipSet`, `ClipSource`, `RestBody`, `ClipWarning`, `Clip`, `ClipKey`, `KeyPose`, `LimbPose`, `ClipEvent`, `ClipContact`, `BakedClip`, `RetargetOptions`, `AnimationClipJSON`, `ClipLibrary`, `ClipOrigin`, `ClipLayerOptions`, `ClipPlay`, `ClipSlot`, `ClipLayerState`, `ClipLayerEvent`, `ClipStepInput`.\n307\t\n308\t**Moves (WP 6.1)**: moves as data in metres (the kind move: my-3d2dge's 24, converted by Appendix C's table), timelines whose events carry leftover time, attacks and combos with hit windows, sweeps and root motion, moves played on a humanoid through its body state (the arm path), moves at a moment for previews, measured hit shapes, the cone helpers and a hit's knockback velocity.\n309\t`defineMove`, `getMove`, `resolveMove`, `MOVE_PHASES`, `LEGACY_FIELDS`, `fromLegacy`, `Timeline`, `Attack`, `Combo`, `moveAt`, `measureMove`, `strikePart`, `strikeSegment`, `inArc`, `inHitShape`, `knockbackVelocity`; types `Move`, `MoveSpec`, `MoveInput`, `MovePhase`, `LegacyMove`, `TimelinePhase`, `TimelineMark`, `TimelineEvent`, `TimelineState`, `AttackState`, `ComboState`, `Sweep`, `HitShape`, `StrikeBody`, `StrikeAnimator`, `ArmState`.\n310\t\n311\t**Reactions and secondary motion (WP 6.2)**: a directional flinch, knockdown, death and the get-up chosen by how the body lies (face up or face down), stepped in the sim; verlet chains (hair, tails) with bone-capsule colliders, a floor probe and a rest shape, a cape of two chains, and the squash spring as a volume-preserving root scale, drawn by an animator layer in the view.\n312\t`Reactions`, `REACTION`, `REACTIONS`, `lyingOf`, `Chain`, `CHAIN`, `Cape`, `Secondary`, `HAIR`, `CAPE`, `TAIL`, `SQUASH`, `squashScale`, `BODY_CAPSULES`, `bodyCapsules`; types `ReactionOptions`, `ReactionState`, `ReactionBody`, `ReactionName`, `Lying`, `ChainOptions`, `ChainTuning`, `BoneCapsule`, `CapeOptions`, `SecondaryOptions`.\n313\t\n314\t**Animation checks (WP 6.6)**: the state matrix (each body state held at each facing, from rest and back to rest) and animCheckBody, which checks a whole body over it: every pose, the same hashes twice, 1/60 s against 1/120 s; and the camera presets sheets of it are drawn from.\n315\t`stateMatrix`, `STATE_MATRIX`, `animCheckBody`, `SHEET_CAMERAS`; types `MatrixState`, `MatrixRun`, `StateMatrixOptions`, `BodyCheckOptions`, `BodyCheckReport`, `AnimCheckName`.\n316\t\n317\t**Actions (WP 8.7)**: multi-beat sequences a body plays, beats of body states, clips, IK targets, flips and squashes with held contact frames, events, flags and cancel windows; the engine's set is data/anim/actions/, which loadActions defines.\n318\t`Actions`, `defineAction`, `getAction`, `loadActions`, `actionCheck`, `ACTION`, `FLIP_AXES`, `EFFECTORS`, `CHANNELS`; types `ActionsOptions`, `ActionsState`, `Action`, `Beat`, `ActionSpec`.\n319\t\n320\t**Clip sets in the registry (WP 8.9)**: the kind clipset, one entry per set of readable clips, which a page or tool registers from data/anim/sets/ (defineClipSet), and the clip layer of an entity kept as plain data, loaded from it at every use (ClipLayerPool), as an actor's component clips holds it.\n321\t`defineClipSet`, `clipSetFromFiles`, `getClipSet`, `clipSetId`, `clipSetIds`, `defineSetBuild`, `CLIPSET_FIELDS`, `CLIPS_FIELDS`, `emptyClipState`, `ClipLayerPool`; types `ClipSetEntry`, `ClipsData`.\n322\t\n323\t**The quadruped rig (WP 13.21)**: a dog on the planted gait for a crowd body (the rig `quadruped`), its state plain data, its pose the joints its body is drawn on, and its rig contract for the anim QA.\n324\t`Quadruped`, `QUADRUPED`, `QUADRUPED_LEGS`, `QUADRUPED_RIG`; types `QuadrupedOptions`, `QuadrupedInput`, `QuadrupedState`, `QuadrupedPose`, `QuadrupedBone`.\n325\t\n326\t**Physics (WP 3.1)**: Rapier behind plain terms. A scene with `physics: true` has a physics world: bodies by id, raycasts, shape casts, overlaps and ground probes, collision groups by name, a level's bodies; presentation reads it through the read-only QueryView.\n327\t`physicsOf`, `COLLISION_GROUPS`, `PHYSICS_SETTINGS`, `addLevelBodies`, `queryViewOf`; types `Physics`, `BodySpec`, `ColliderSpec`, `BodyState`, `BodyChange`, `BodyType`, `Shape`, `Vec3Like`, `QuatLike`, `Hit`, `Ground`, `QueryOptions`, `RayOptions`, `ShapeCastOptions`, `OverlapOptions`, `ProbeOptions`, `GroupName`, `QueryView`.\n328\t\n329\t**Characters (WP 3.2)**: actors moved by the character controller (Rapier's, moved across then down), with an action game's feel: acceleration and skid, coyote time, a jump buffer, a variable jump, a wall jump, a dash, pushing props; the feel meter measures how one handles. A body's reactions and an actor's secondary motion as components (WP 7.8), and its actions (`actOn`, WP 13.11).\n330\t`createCharacter`, `CHARACTER_FIELDS`, `CHARACTER_STATE_FIELDS`, `spawnActor`, `removeActor`, `measureFeel`, `reactTo`, `stepReactions`, `REACTION_FIELDS`, `SECONDARY_FIELDS`, `actOn`, `ACTIONS_FIELDS`; types `Character`, `CharacterState`, `ActorSpec`, `ActorComponent`, `ActorComponents`, `FeelReport`, `FeelOptions`, `AnimatorComponent`, `SecondaryComponent`, `ReactionComponents`, `ActionComponents`.\n331\t\n332\t**Levels v2 (WP 4.2)**: the hall's glyphs (gallery =, stairs ^, dais o, brazier t), ramps and daises, hull and cylinder colliders, and the surface tag underfoot.\n333\t`surfaceAt`; types `HullCollider`, `CylinderCollider`, `RampDescriptor`, `DaisDescriptor`, `Side`.\n334\t\n335\t**Navigation and spatial queries (WP 4.3)**: a level's walkable grid under a climb limit (the crowd's, or a hero's jump reach from measureFeel), A* paths smoothed by line of sight, cached flow fields with reach and a look-ahead direction, and a spatial hash on typed arrays for neighbours.\n336\t`NavGrid`, `FlowField`, `NAV_GRID_FIELDS`, `NAV_DIRECTIONS`, `LOOK_AHEAD`, `NEAREST_RINGS`, `NAV_CLIMB`, `SpatialHash`, `SPATIAL_HASH_FIELDS`; types `NavGridOptions`, `FlowOptions`, `GroundPoint`, `SpatialHashOptions`.\n337\t\n338\t**Bodies and props (WP 4.1)**: props of one shape in the prototype's material, torque impulses and the knock; movers, kinematic bodies whose pose is a function of time, carrying riders; sensors and triggers; the lost-body rescue.\n339\t`PROP_MATERIAL`, `addProp`, `addBox`, `addBall`, `addCapsule`, `addCylinder`, `applyTorqueImpulse`, `knock`, `MOVER_FIELDS`, `MOVER`, `moverPose`, `driveBody`, `driveMover`, `addSensor`, `sensorHits`, `TRIGGER_FIELDS`, `updateTrigger`, `rescueLost`; types `PropSpec`, `KnockOptions`, `Mover`, `SensorSpec`, `Trigger`, `RescueLevel`.\n340\t\n341\t**Props as data (WP 4.5)**: the registry kind `prop` (meshes by geometry and material id, a body, a light spot, where a level places it, the hit and use hooks), the engine's crate, barrel, brazier fire and pillar, the library's sconce, banner, chest, door, table, bench, statue and lantern (WP 10.3), and their placement.\n342\t`defineProp`, `getProp`, `propCentre`, `levelProps`, `propBody`, `propPose`; types `Prop`, `PropDefinition`, `PropPart`, `PropTouch`, `PropKnock`, `PropPlacement`, `PropBody`, `PropPose`.\n343\t\n344\t**Props in a scene (WP 4.6)**: spawnProp stands a prop in a running scene (its body, and an entity with the component sceneProp whose pose is read back after each step, which the view draws as the prop); hitProp calls its hit hook with the means to knock it; removeProp.\n345\t`spawnProp`, `removeProp`, `hitProp`, `SCENE_PROP_FIELDS`; types `ScenePropComponent`, `ScenePropComponents`.\n346\t\n347\t**Crowd bodies (WP 5.1)**: agents as dynamic capsules and balls driven by the velocities their AI wants (approach limits, only on their feet), knockback and launches, the corpse group and its timers, spawn and despawn queues.\n348\t`CROWD_AGENT_FIELDS`, `CROWD_AGENT`, `CROWD_MATERIAL`, `CROWD_LIMITS`, `CROWD_GROUNDED`, `addAgent`, `removeAgent`, `setVelocity`, `knockback`, `launch`, `killAgent`, `updateCrowd`; types `CrowdAgent`, `AgentState`, `VelocityOptions`, `CrowdOptions`, `CrowdChanges`, `CrowdStepper`.\n349\t\n350\t**Steering kit (WP 5.2)**: the velocity a crowd agent wants (seek and arrive, flow-field following, approach rings, a flyer's hover, a jumper's hop), attack tokens that cap the attackers at once, and awareness by distance and sight.\n351\t`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`, `hover`, `airTime`, `hop`, `FOLLOW`, `APPROACH_RING`, `HOVER`, `HOP`, `ATTACK_TOKENS_FIELDS`, `ATTACK_TOKENS`, `takeToken`, `releaseToken`, `holdsToken`, `keepTokens`, `canSee`, `clearSight`, `SIGHT`; types `SteerPoint`, `FollowOptions`, `ApproachRingOptions`, `HoverOptions`, `AttackTokens`, `SightOptions`.\n352\t\n353\t**Crowd bodies (WP 5.7)**: a crowd member's look and rig (a humanoid in a build, a blob, a floater) in the component crowdBody, its rig stepped in the sim by stepCrowdBody; the stage draws them through a crowd with its level of detail.\n354\t`CROWD_BODY_FIELDS`, `CROWD_BODY`, `stepCrowdBody`; types `CrowdBody`, `CrowdRig`, `CrowdBodyInput`.\n355\t\n356\t**Telegraphs (WP 6.4)**: an attack's warning shape on the floor as data (an arc, a ring, a line or a cone, in metres), and the geometry sim code tests it with as it goes off: a body inside it is hit, one just outside it a near miss.\n357\t`TELEGRAPH_SHAPES`, `defineTelegraph`, `getTelegraph`, `telegraphGap`, `inTelegraph`, `nearMiss`, `telegraphFill`, `coneOf`; types `Telegraph`, `TelegraphSpec`, `TelegraphPlace`, `PointLike`.\n358\t\n359\t**Combat helpers (WP 6.3)**: hit queries against hurt capsules found through a spatial hash (cones with height windows, weapon sweeps), damage on health and crowd bodies (knockback as momentum, launches, death into the corpse group, the event damage), hit-stop handed to the frame clock's budget, and projectiles with swept tests.\n360\t`PROJECTILE_FIELDS`, `PROJECTILE`, `stepProjectiles`, `capsuleCast`, `inCone`, `coneHits`, `sweepTouches`, `sweepHits`, `physicsWalls`, `HEALTH_FIELDS`, `HEALTH`, `damage`, `requestHitStop`; types `Projectile`, `ProjectileState`, `ProjectileStep`, `ProjectileHit`, `HurtCapsule`, `HurtSet`, `WallTest`, `WallContact`, `Health`, `DamageTarget`, `HitSpec`, `DamageEvent`, `DamageEvents`.\n361\t\n362\t**Melee (WP 13.19)**: Apex's punch, kicks and the door kick, a lunge to the target in reach, one hit a swing landing at its moment with its damage and hit-stop; the fan of rays that finds what a strike reaches.\n363\t`MELEE`, `MELEE_MOVES`, `MELEE_FIELDS`, `addMelee`, `meleeTargets`, `fanHits`; types `Me