t replaces that number in every model character's model, from the next step), and an actor placed at rest (a scene's dev action: a feel course's start).\n384\t`MOVEMENT_SETTINGS`, `movementOverrides`, `tuneCharacter`, `placeActor`; types `MovementOverride`.\n385\t\n386\t**Rides (WP 13.7)**: ziplines, ladders and launchers as world data (engine/world/traverse.ts), placed in a scene (engine/sim/rides.ts) and ridden by the shooter model (engine/physics/movement/ride.ts, ladder.ts): a zipline and the poles under its anchors, the redeploy balloon's line and its skydive, a ladder (or every ladder a level's ladder glyph `|` stands), a jump pad or a trampoline. Use is the button `interact`; the look's pitch picks a vertical zipline's way and a skydive's dive.\n387\t`addZipline`, `addLadder`, `addLauncher`, `addLevelLadders`, `ridesOf`, `levelLadders`; types `ZiplineSpec`, `LadderSpec`, `LauncherSpec`, `Rides`, `RideCable`, `RideLadder`, `RideLauncher`.\n388\t\n389\t**Storeys (WP 13.13)**: format 2, levels written as storeys, read a storey at a time or in 3D: the highest surface at or under a point, its storey (by height, or by that surface) and its room.\n390\t`LEVEL_FORMAT_2`, `storeyView`, `floorBelow`, `surfaceBelow`, `storeyAt`, `storeyBelow`, `roomAt`; types `StoreyDescriptor`, `RoomDescriptor`, `OpeningDescriptor`, `RoofDescriptor`.\n391\t\n392\t**Terrain (WP 10.5)**: the registry kind `terrain`, a seeded heightfield, its queries, its body and its navigation.\n393\t`defineTerrain`, `getTerrain`, `makeTerrain`, `terrainProblems`, `TERRAIN_FIELDS`, `terrainHeight`, `terrainGradient`, `terrainNormal`, `terrainGrade`, `terrainSurface`, `scatterTerrain`, `addTerrainBody`, `terrainNavGrid`, `terrainNavSurface`; types `Terrain`, `TerrainSpec`, `TerrainEntry`, `TerrainHill`, `TerrainFlat`, `ScatterRule`, `TerrainPlace`, `TerrainNavOptions`, `NavSurface`, `NavSource`.\n394\t\n395\t**Sites (WP 13.15)**: levels standing on a terrain, each placed and turned in quarter turns, the terrain cut under them; the queries over all of it, the bodies, the ladders and the navigation over every storey and the terrain.\n396\t`defineSite`, `getSite`, `makeSite`, `SITE_FIELDS`, `toWorld`, `toLocal`, `siteFloorBelow`, `siteSurfaceBelow`, `siteStructureAt`, `siteSolidAt`, `siteClearSight`, `siteRescueGrid`, `createSiteNav`, `addSiteBodies`, `addSiteLadders`; types `Site`, `SiteSpec`, `SiteStructure`, `StructureSpec`, `SiteRescueGrid`, `SiteNav`, `SiteNavOptions`.\n397\t\n398\t**The scene's props as navigation blocks (WP 13.21)**: what a site's walkers go round.\n399\t`propObstacles`; types `NavBlock`.\n400\t\n401\t**Use (WP 13.16)**: the system `interact` (a player's use of the thing in reach and in view, with its prompt; carrying and throwing a light prop), the kinds `usable` and `strikable`, and the body-to-entity lookup.\n402\t`addInteraction`, `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `strikeEntity`, `USE_REACH`, `CARRY_MASS`, `THROW_SPEED`, `propOfBody`; types `UsableSpec`, `InteractorComponent`, `StrikeForce`, `SpawnPropOptions`, `PropHealthComponent`, `PropLightComponent`.\n403\t\n404\t**Doors (WP 13.16)**: hung on a hinge or sliding, opened away from the user, locked, blocked, burst open and broken.\n405\t`addDoor`, `addLevelDoors`, `useDoor`, `strikeDoor`, `breakDoor`, `DOOR_SWING`; types `DoorSpec`, `DoorComponent`.\n406\t\n407\t**Windows and breakables (WP 13.16)**: props with health and their breaking, a level's window panes, a bolt's strike.\n408\t`addLevelWindows`, `addBreakables`, `damageProp`, `breakProp`, `strikeBody`, `BREAK_SPEED`.\n409\t\n410\t**Switches (WP 13.16)**: a room's lights and the garage door, as sim state the view reads.\n411\t`addSwitch`, `switchGroup`, `flipSwitch`; types `SwitchSpec`, `SwitchComponent`.\n412\t\n413\t**Strikes (WP 13.19)**: a strikable entry by entity (the move a melee plays on it: a door's kick), what takes one, and whose a body is (a prop's or an actor's, WP 13.20).\n414\t`strikeEntry`, `takesStrike`, `entityOfBody`; types `StrikeEntry`.\n415\t\n416\t**Light and time (WP 13.23)**: the world's clock, and the light groups it switches at dusk and dawn.\n417\t`setDaytime`, `daytimeOf`, `lightsAtDusk`, `litAt`, `DUSK`, `DAWN`; types `DaytimeComponent`, `DuskComponent`.\n418\t\n419\t**Things (WP 10.4)**: what can be hit, as a range's targets and a ring's dummies: the kind `thing` (its prop, hit points, rise, track and hooks), a thing's state (hits, down and up, its place on its track), and things in a scene (spawned as their prop, struck as hits, stood back up and driven along their tracks by the system `things`).\n420\t`defineThing`, `getThing`, `thingState`, `hitThing`, `stepThing`, `thingAt`, `THING_FIELDS`, `THING_KIND_FIELDS`, `spawnThing`, `strikeThing`; types `Thing`, `ThingKind`, `ThingSpec`, `ThingTouch`, `ThingHit`, `ThingOutcome`, `ThingStanding`.\n421\t\n422\t**Projectile patterns (WP 10.4)**: a shot emitter's volleys (aimed, forward, ring, spiral, random) and timing (delay, interval, bursts and their pause, range), and the shots it looses over WP 6.3's projectiles.\n423\t`SHOT_EMITTER`, `SHOT_EMITTER_FIELDS`, `PATTERNS`, `MOST_VOLLEYS`, `volley`, `stepVolleys`, `loose`, `direction`, `headingTo`; types `ShotEmitter`, `Pattern`, `VolleyAim`, `LooseOptions`.\n424\t\n425\t**Awareness (WP 10.4)**: beyond WP 5.2's sight, the noises an agent hears and its memory of where it last saw or heard.\n426\t`AWARENESS`, `AWARENESS_FIELDS`, `AWARENESS_STATES`, `sense`, `hears`, `earshot`; types `Awareness`, `AwarenessState`, `Noise`, `SenseInput`, `Sensed`.\n427\t\n428\t**Springs and blends (WP 10.1)**: numbers, vectors and turns on exact damped springs with kicks; named weights eased up and down; the pose stack, angles averaged round the circle, a pitch and a yaw as a direction.\n429\t`Spring1`, `Spring3`, `SpringQuat`, `StateWeights`, `layerPose`, `blendAngles`, `aimDirection`; types `SpringOptions`, `BlendRates`, `PoseLayer`.\n430\t\n431\t**Legs and bodies (WP 10.1)**: the digitigrade leg's reverse knee; sway chains and lagging parts; the planted gait for N legs in groups, its feet on raycast ground or gripping walls; path chains following their head.\n432\t`digitigrade`, `SwayChain`, `LagPart`, `LegGait`, `PathChain`; types `DigitigradeLeg`, `DigitigradeFrame`, `DigitigradeSolve`, `SwayOptions`, `LagOptions`, `GaitLeg`, `GaitOptions`, `GaitFoot`, `GaitProbe`, `GaitBody`, `PathOptions`.\n433\t\n434\t**Faces and floating parts (WP 10.1)**: looking within bounds and blinking from a named stream; hovering, banking, leaning and folding; orbiters and detached parts; pose history played back late; extras on sockets; tip trails.\n435\t`LookAt`, `Blink`, `Hover`, `Orbiters`, `DetachedPart`, `PoseHistory`, `socketMatrix`, `placeExtras`, `along`, `alongDirection`, `TipTrail`; types `LookAtOptions`, `BlinkOptions`, `HoveringOptions`, `OrbitOptions`, `DetachedOptions`, `HistoryOptions`, `Socket`, `SocketJoints`, `TrailOptions`, `TipSample`.\n436\t\n437\t**Reach and the rig contract (WP 10.1)**: an attack's reach measured off its animation; what a procedural rig names (its points, bones, feet and states) and the check it passes, as anim.check holds the humanoid.\n438\t`measureReach`, `driveRig`, `rigCheck`, `rigClosed`; types `ReachPose`, `ReachOptions`, `Reach`, `ProcRig`, `ProcRigBody`, `RigRun`.\n439\t\n440\t**Footsteps (WP 13.22)**: which step a surface tag plays at a gait, sound data a scene's sound map reads sim-side.\n441\t`footstepSound`, `stepGait`, `SURFACE_STEPS`, `STEP_GAITS`; types `StepGait`.\n442\t\n443\tPages only, from engine/index.ts:\n444\t\n445\t**Headless**: the engine without a page, and its inspector (window.__engine in a page).\n446\t`createHeadless`; types `Headless`, `HeadlessOptions`, `Inspector`, `EngineInfo`, `EntitySummary`.\n447\t\n448\t**Materials and procedural textures (WP 2.3)**: materials as data with three.js's classic parameters, seeded tiling texture generators and their helpers, the CPU bake, and the page's material library.\n449\t`defineMaterial`, `getMaterial`, `createMaterialLibrary`, `defineTexture`, `bakeTexture`, `cellHash`, `wrapIndex`, `palette`, `pickTone`, `runningBond`, `tileNoise`; types `MaterialEntry`, `MaterialSpec`, `MaterialLibrary`, `TextureEntry`, `Texel`, `TextureContext`, `TextureSpec`, `TextureLook`, `TextureBake`, `TextureRef`.\n450\t\n451\t**Input (WP 3.3)**: the devices of a page sampled into intents (keys by physical code, mouse with pointer lock and the wheel, gamepads in any slot), action maps and their presets, bindings players change and keep, prompts per device, the virtual device that __engine.input drives, and the scripted gamepad for tests.\n452\t`createInput`, `defineActionMap`, `getActionMap`, `listActionMaps`, `SYSTEM_ACTIONS`, `RESERVED_CODES`, `createBindings`, `codeLabel`, `readKeyboardLayout`, `isCode`, `createVirtualDevice`, `connectVirtualDevice`, `createScriptedGamepad`; types `Input`, `InputOptions`, `CameraLike`, `CameraInputSample`, `ActionMap`, `ActionMapSpec`, `Bindings`, `BindingsOptions`, `BindingsStorage`, `BindingsFile`, `RebindResult`, `DeviceKind`, `VirtualDevice`, `ScriptedGamepad`, `GamepadLike`.\n453\t\n454\t**Aiming and look (WP 13.9)**: the point the intents' aim carries by a map's rule, and the look settings input.*.\n455\t`aimPoint`, `screenRay`, `onFloor`, `AIM_RULES`, `AIM_RANGE`, `STICK_REACH`, `lookOptions`, `scaleLook`, `LOOK_SETTING_FIELDS`, `LOOK_SETTINGS`, `DEGREES_PER_COUNT`; types `AimRule`, `AimPose`, `AimView`, `AimDevices`, `AimRay`, `LookOptions`, `RawLook`, `ScaledLook`.\n456\t\n457\t**Held bindings (WP 13.20)**: a key or a button kept down for an action of its own (Apex's pad reload on X held), and the device a code is of.\n458\t`holdCode`, `heldBase`, `HOLD_PREFIX`, `HOLD_TIME`, `deviceOf`.\n459\t\n460\t**Geometry kit and level meshes (WP 2.4)**: shapes standing on the ground with UVs in metres, the geometry registry (props and levels name geometry by id), their QA measures, and a compiled level drawn as meshes.\n461\t`boxGeometry`, `roundedBoxGeometry`, `limbGeometry`, `rampGeometry`, `frustumGeometry`, `pillarGeometry`, `pillarParts`, `ringGeometry`, `sidesThenTops`, `mergeGeometries`, `defineGeometry`, `getGeometry`, `buildGeometry`, `inspectGeometry`, `createLevelMeshes`, `LEVEL_RECIPES`, `createTerrainMeshes`; types `BoxOptions`, `RoundedBoxOptions`, `LimbOptions`, `RampOptions`, `FrustumOptions`, `PillarOptions`, `PillarPart`, `RingOptions`, `GeometryEntry`, `GeometrySpec`, `GeometryRef`, `GeometryReport`, `LevelMeshes`, `LevelMeshesOptions`, `LevelMeshPart`, `LevelCell`, `LevelRecipe`, `LevelShape`, `TerrainMeshes`, `TerrainMeshesOptions`.\n462\t\n463\t**Sound data (WP 9.2)**: effects and songs as registry entries (voices, layers, variants; tracker-notation tracks), rendered offline by the DSP core into reproducible samples.\n464\t`defineSfx`, `getSfx`, `renderSfx`, `sfxVoices`, `defineSong`, `getSong`, `renderSong`, `songVoices`, `songTiming`; types `Sfx`, `SfxSpec`, `SfxLayer`, `SfxBus`, `SfxRenderOptions`, `Song`, `SongSpec`, `SongTrack`, `SongTiming`, `SongRenderOptions`, `Voice`, `SoundDef`.\n465\t\n466\t**The audio runtime (WP 9.3)**: a page's sound through Web Audio, started by the first gesture: buses with ducking and voice limits, sounds placed in 3D and occluded through the QueryView, room reverb, and music in intensity layers that change on the beat and the bar line.\n467\t`createAudio`, `BUS_VOLUMES`, `ROOMS`, `roomReverb`, `VOICE_LIMITS`; types `Audio`, `AudioOptions`, `AudioState`, `AudioInfo`, `PlayOptions`, `Sound`, `ListenerPose`, `GestureTarget`, `AudioBus`, `MixerInfo`, `MusicChange`, `MusicPosition`, `Quantize`, `RoomName`, `RoomSpec`, `VoiceLimits`.\n468\t\n469\t**Sound for places (WP 13.22)**: a room's reverb out of its size and what it is made of (Sabine's RT60), the walls between a sound and the listener counted, each muffling it more.\n470\t`roomAcoustics`, `ABSORPTION`, `CONTENTS`, `wallsBetween`, `muffling`, `OCCLUSION`; types `RoomMake`.\n471\t\n472\t**The engine in a page (WP 2.7)**: createEngine reads the routes, starts the scene with its view, publishes window.__engine, warms up and runs the frame loop; createHeadless({ view: true }) builds the same view in Node.\n473\t`createEngine`, `parseRoutes`, `routeQuery`; types `Engine`, `EngineOptions`, `Routes`, `CameraInfo`, `PlayResult`, `PlayIntoOptions`, `ViewChange`.\n474\t\n475\t**A page's own presentation (WP 9.5)**: createEngine's onFrame, once per drawn frame, with the run to read and the camera.\n476\ttypes `EngineFrame`, `FrameWorld`, `CameraPose`.\n477\t\n478\t**Touch controls (WP 3.12; built, not tested, ADR-0021)**: a page's touch layout (the corner fan's buttons and the menu button) for createEngine's touchLayout and onMenu, the touch device on input.touch, and the touch codes it writes.\n479\t`defineTouchLayout`, `getTouchLayout`, `touchCode`; types `TouchLayout`, `TouchLayoutSpec`, `TouchButtonSpec`, `TouchDevice`, `StickView`, `TouchSetting`.\n480\t\n481\t**Level meshes v2 and the cutaway (WP 4.4)**: galleries, stairs, the dais, pillars with plinths and capitals and brazier stands as recipe shapes; walls facing an outside camera cut low and pillars before the hero as stumps.\n482\t`createCutaway`, `planCutaway`; types `Cutaway`, `CutawayReport`, `CutTarget`, `LevelCut`, `CutWhen`, `PartCut`.\n483\t\n484\t**Instancing and puppets (WP 3.8)**: batches of instanced copies in pages above r182's 1,000-slot uniform path; puppet bodies as data (one line per part on the humanoid skeleton's bones, mirrored parts, material slots), compiled per build and drawn as one rigid-skinned mesh per character or as instanced parts for crowds; blob shadows.\n485\t`createInstancedBatch`, `defineBody`, `getBody`, `compileBody`, `recolor`, `createPuppet`, `createPuppetBatches`, `createBlobShadows`, `blobScale`; types `InstancedBatch`, `InstancedBatchOptions`, `InstancedBatchStats`, `InstanceColor`, `Body`, `BodySpec`, `BodyPart`, `BodyPoint`, `BodyLength`, `PartOptions`, `CompiledBody`, `BodyPiece`, `PieceShape`, `Puppet`, `PuppetMaterials`, `PuppetBatches`, `BlobShadows`.\n486\t\n487\t**The HTML/CSS overlay (WP 6.5)**: world-anchored labels and damage numbers placed from projected coordinates each frame (three.js's CSS2DRenderer pattern), HUD lines and a crosshair over the canvas; ui.state() for tests.\n488\t`createOverlay`, `DAMAGE_NUMBERS`, `uiState`; types `Overlay`, `OverlayOptions`, `HudOptions`, `HudPlace`, `Label`, `LabelOptions`, `DamageOptions`, `WorldPoint`, `Anchor`, `OverlayCamera`, `UiState`, `UiElementState`.\n489\t\n490\t**The widgets (WP 10.6)**: bars, hearts and boxes, the dialog box and the menu as HTML/CSS over the overlay, reading the page's input (its system layer) and asking the page for sound cues; a gamepad's cursor over any panel. A menu sends the picks that change play as input (`send`), so replays hold them.\n491\t`createBar`, `createHearts`, `createBox`, `createPadCursor`, `createDialog`, `createMenu`.\n492\t\n493\t**The store (WP 10.9)**: what a game keeps across sessions (high scores, progress, its settings) in the page's localStorage under the game's prefix, versioned and checked on load; memory in Node and in the isolated modes.\n494\t`createStore`, `memoryBackend`.\n495\t\n496\t**Lights, shadows, atmosphere and sky (WP 7.1)**: light kinds for a level's light spots, a fixed pool of point lights near the focus with a shadow budget by quality tier, and sky presets (background, dome, backdrop, sun, ambient light, fog) that a sky puts on a scene.\n497\t`defineLight`, `defineSky`, `createSky`; types `LightEntry`, `LightSource`, `SkyEntry`, `SkySpec`, `Sky`.\n498\t\n499\t**Materials v2 (WP 7.2)**: the same material data drawn in four styles (lambert, toon, flat, pbr), switched at run time with every style warmed; the toon look (bands and rim) as uniforms; outline shells for meshes and instanced batches.\n500\t`createStyleSet`, `MATERIAL_STYLES`, `TOON_LOOK`, `createOutline`, `OUTLINE_MODES`; types `MaterialStyle`, `StyleSet`, `StyleSetOptions`, `ToonLook`, `ToonUniforms`, `Outline`, `OutlineMode`, `OutlineOptions`, `OutlineShells`.\n501\t\n502\t**Post-processing (WP 7.4, 13.32)**: one chain every frame draws through, every filter a uniform with an amount, scoped by part; looks as data (a material style, a filter chain, the sky, WP 7.8) that combine looks; objects tagged by part.\n503\t`createPost`, `POST_SETTINGS`, `definePostSettings`, `setPart`, `PARTS`, `defineLook`, `FILTER_FIELDS`, `FILTER_NAMES`, `SCOPES`, `PALETTES`, `STYLIZE_KINDS`; types `Post`, `PostOptions`, `Part`, `PostTag`, `LookEntry`, `LookSpec`, `FilterName`, `Scope`, `FilterChain`, `FilterSpec`, `CombineLayer`, `PostShow`.\n504\t\n505\t**Props drawn (WP 4.5)**: every prop part a batch of the instancing service, posed by its body, the flames dancing on the visual streams, the light spots flickering, for the light pool; a part's geometry and where it stands (its matrix in the prop's frame), for a prop's tests.\n506\t`createPropMeshes`, `FLICKER`, `buildPropPart`, `partMatrix`; types `PropMeshes`, `PropMeshesOptions`, `PropMeshPose`, `PropLight`.\n507\t\n508\t**Crowd rendering and pose LOD (WP 5.4)**: thousands of humanoids, blobs and floaters in a few dozen draw calls over the instancing service, each body seen through bands of detail by its distance (the animator's pose level, capes), culled by the view; the blob and floater bodies as data.\n509\t`createCrowd`, `createCrowdLod`, `CROWD_LOD`, `BLOB_BODY`, `blobBody`, `FLOATER_BODY`, `QUADRUPED_BODY`; types `Crowd`, `CrowdDraw`, `CrowdRigDraw`, `CrowdStats`, `RigBody`, `RigPart`, `RigJoints`, `RigShape`, `CrowdLod`, `CrowdLodOptions`, `CrowdLodCounts`, `CrowdBand`, `CrowdBandSpec`, `BlobFeatures`.\n510\t\n511\t**Performance counters (WP 5.5)**: what `__engine.stats()` returns, means in ms and the renderer's counts, never fps.\n512\ttypes `EngineStats`.\n513\t\n514\t**The texture library (v2, WP 7.3)**: a level's whole floor as a texture generator (define it under a game's id), with moss at the walls' feet, a rune circle on each dais and contact shadows along every block's foot.\n515\t`levelFloorTexture`; types `LevelFloorOptions`, `ContactShadowOptions`, `FloorMossOptions`, `FloorRuneOptions`.\n516\t\n517\t**Clips in pages (WP 8.4)**: three.js's AnimationClip and AnimationMixer, for presentation only (a page plays a baked clip on a puppet with clipJSON and AnimationClip.parse); the sim samples the same tracks through the clip layer. The mannequin: the Quaternius figure the library was measured on, as a body and a build.\n518\t`AnimationClip`, `AnimationMixer`, `defineMannequin`, `mannequinBody`, `MANNEQUIN_SLOTS`; types `MannequinData`.\n519\t\n520\t**Effects (WP 6.4)**: particles as data (emitters: shardfall's presets, the 2D engine's kinds, event effects), drawn with telegraphs on the floor, socket trails, bolts and camera shake by a view's effects; never part of the hash.\n521\t`createEffects`, `FX_BUDGET`, `BOLT`, `defineEmitter`, `getEmitter`, `TRAIL_TINTS`, `ScreenShake`, `SHAKE`; types `Effects`, `EffectsOptions`, `FxEventPayload`, `FxStats`, `Emitter`, `EmitterSpec`, `EmitterLayer`, `ParticleFields`, `FxLight`, `BurstOptions`, `ParticleStats`, `TrailSampleLike`.\n522\t\n523\t**The feel kit (WP 13.4)**: courses, scripted runs of a scene and the numbers measured on them (`node x feel`); the runner and its verdicts; the owner's feel panel and copy as profile.\n524\t`defineCourse`, `getCourse`, `listCourses`, `METRICS`, `runCourse`, `createFeelPanel`, `tunedSpec`, `profileText`; types `Course`, `Measure`, `Metric`, `CourseResult`, `CourseSample`, `MeasureResult`, `FeelPanel`, `FeelRow`, `FeelInspector`.\n525\t\n526\t**Camera modes (WP 2.5, WP 3.9, WP 13.8)**: a code to a camera, each mode's factory, and a game's own.\n527\t`readCameraCode`, `createCamera`, `createShoulderCamera`, `SHOULDER`, `createFollowCamera`, `FOLLOW_RIG`, `createFirstCamera`, `createChaseCamera`, `createViewCamera`, `followCamera`, `defineCamera`, `makePose`, `poseForward`, `poseRight`, `poseUp`, `poseTarget`; types `AnyCamera`, `AnyCameraSpec`, `ShoulderInput`, `ShoulderSpec`, `FollowInput`, `FollowSpec`, `FollowCamera`, `FollowSteps`, `CameraKindSpec`, `Camera`, `CameraInput`.\n528\t\n529\t**The rig a view steps (WP 13.8)**: blends on live switches, the view effects, the view settings cam.*.\n530\t`createCameraRig`, `rigOptions`, `CAMERA_SETTINGS`, `blendCameras`, `BLEND_TIME`, `createCameraEffects`, `applyEffects`, `cameraPlayer`, `apexFov`, `EFFECTS`; types `CameraRig`, `RigOptions`, `RigInput`, `CameraPlayer`, `EffectWeights`, `EffectsFrame`.\n531\t<!-- api-map:end -->\n532\t/**\n533\t * @file The starter game's page (labs/game/index.html): it starts the engine (`createEngine`, engine/app/engine.ts) on\n534\t * the scene `game:play` (labs/game/scenes/play.ts) with the arena's text registered for the view, offers the bot's\n535\t * replay to `?replay=bot`, and shows the round each drawn frame in the HTML/CSS overlay's HUD: the gems, the clock,\n536\t * the controls, the best round kept across sessions (labs/game/best.ts, through the engine's store) and, when the\n537\t * round ends, how it ended. Enter then starts a new round on the next seed. Escape (or the pad's Start) pauses the\n538\t * round under a menu (`createMenu`: Resume, New round), which keeps its keys out of play while it is open\n539\t * (`createEngine({ modal })`); the store keeps the best round and any keys rebound (`createEngine({ store })`). The\n540\t * camera is the chase camera unless the URL names one (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=orbit`).\n541\t *\n542\t * Everything else a page does (routes, the renderer, the frame loop, the devices, `window.__engine`, the error\n543\t * overlay, the touch controls) is the engine's, so this file is the game's own presentation only. It reads the run\n544\t * and never changes it: the HUD cannot change the hash.\n545\t *\n546\t * Game code: it imports only the engine's public API (engine/index.ts) and the game's own files.\n547\t *\n548\t * Open it at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (`npm run dev`): `?seed=7&game.gems=20` plays another round,\n549\t * `?replay=bot` the bot's, `?cam=iso` another camera. Headless, `node x shot labs/game` reports what it draws.\n550\t */\n551\t/// <reference types=\"vite/client\" />\n552\timport { createEngine, createMenu, createStore, type Engine, type EngineFrame, type Menu } from '../../engine';\n553\timport { bestOf, recordBest } from './best';\n554\timport { ARENA_TEXT, play, type Round } from './scenes/play';\n555\t\n556\t/** The page's query, with the chase camera when it names none: it follows the player round the arena. */\n557\tconst search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\n558\tif (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase');\n559\t\n560\t/** The game's replays, by name (`?replay=bot`), as text loaded on demand. */\n561\tconst replays = { bot: () => import('./replays/bot.replay.json?raw').then((module) => module.default) };\n562\t\n563\t/** The started engine; null until `createEngine` resolves (its first frames are drawn before that). */\n564\tlet engine: Engine | null = null;\n565\t\n566\t/** Whether the round on screen has ended, so Enter starts the next. */\n567\tlet ended = false;\n568\t\n569\t/** The game's store: its best round across sessions; a replay's page plays in its demo mode, so it sets none. */\n570\tconst store = createStore({ prefix: 'my3dge-game:', mode: search.has('replay') ? 'demo' : 'play' });\n571\t\n572\t/** The fastest won round kept (s), and the run whose clock the page saw running, with the steps it started with. */\n573\tlet best = bestOf(store);\n574\tlet seen: { world: object; steps: number } | null = null;\n575\t\n576\t/** The pause menu while it is open; null while the round plays. */\n577\tlet paused: Menu | null = null;\n578\t\n579\t/** Opens the pause menu over the paused round, or reads it a frame: Resume (or Escape again), New round. */\n580\tfunction pauseMenu(dt: number): void {\n581\t  const [inspector, ui, input] = [engine?.inspector, engine?.ui, engine?.input];\n582\t  if (!inspector || !ui || !input) return;\n583\t  const close = () => {\n584\t    paused?.remove();\n585\t    paused = null;\n586\t    inspector.resume();\n587\t  };\n588\t  if (paused) return void paused.update(dt);\n589\t  if (!input.pressed('pause')) return;\n590\t  inspector.pause();\n591\t  const items = [\n592\t    { label: 'Resume', id: 'resume' },\n593\t    { label: 'New round', id: 'new' },\n594\t  ];\n595\t  paused = createMenu(ui.root, items, { input, id: 'pause', title: 'Paused', onCancel: close, onPick: (_i, item) => {\n596\t    if (typeof item === 'object' && item.id === 'new') inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\n597\t    close();\n598\t  } }); // prettier-ignore\n599\t}\n600\t\n601\t/** Shows the round in the HUD: the gems, the clock, the controls, and how the round ended. */\n602\tfunction showRound({ world }: EngineFrame): void {\n603\t  const ui = engine?.ui;\n604\t  const entity = world.query('round')[0] as unknown as { round: Round } | undefined;\n605\t  if (!ui || !entity) return;\n606\t  const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\n607\t  if (seen?.world !== world) seen = state === 'playing' ? { world, steps: left + world.tick } : null;\n608\t  const was = ended;\n609\t  ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\n610\t  ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color: left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '' });\n611\t  ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash · Esc: pause', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\n612\t  ended = state !== 'playing';\n613\t  if (ended && !was && state === 'won' && seen) best = recordBest(store, (seen.steps - left) / world.hz);\n614\t  ui.hud('best', best === null ? null : `Best ${best.toFixed(1)} s`, { at: 'top-left' });\n615\t  const end = state === 'won' ? 'Every gem! Enter: play again' : 'Time! Enter: play again';\n616\t  ui.hud('end', ended ? end : null, { at: 'top' });\n617\t}\n618\t\n619\tengine = await createEngine({\n620\t  canvas: document.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('#view')!,\n621\t  scenes: [play.id],\n622\t  levels: { 'level:arena': { text: ARENA_TEXT, file: 'labs/game/scenes/play.ts' } },\n623\t  replays,\n624\t  search: `?${search}`,\n625\t  store,\n626\t  modal: () => paused !== null,\n627\t  onFrame: (frame) => {\n628\t    pauseMenu(frame.dt);\n629\t    showRound(frame);\n630\t  },\n631\t});\n632\t\n633\taddEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n634\t  const inspector = engine?.inspector;\n635\t  if (event.code === 'Enter' && ended && inspector) inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\n636\t});\n637\t<!doctype html>\n638\t<html lang=\"en\">\n639\t  <head>\n640\t    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n641\t    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\" />\n642\t    <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#15171c\" />\n643\t    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"../../engine/ui/touch.css\" />\n644\t    <link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:,\" />\n645\t    <title>The starter game: my-3dge</title>\n646\t    <!--\n647\t      The starter game's page (labs/game/README.md): labs/game/main.ts starts the engine on the scene game:play and\n648\t      shows the round in the HUD. Ready or error: until the engine publishes window.__engine, the inline script below\n649\t      records a script that fails to load or throws, and shows it in #msg, so a broken page is one line of text,\n650\t      never a blank one; `node x shot labs/game` reports the same headless.\n651\t    -->\n652\t    <style>\n653\t      html,\n654\t      body {\n655\t        margin: 0;\n656\t        height: 100%;\n657\t        overflow: hidden;\n658\t        background: #15171c;\n659\t      }\n660\t      canvas {\n661\t        display: block;\n662\t        width: 100vw;\n663\t        height: 100vh;\n664\t        height: 100dvh;\n665\t        outline: none;\n666\t        touch-action: none;\n667\t      }\n668\t      #msg {\n669\t        position: fixed;\n670\t        inset: 0;\n671\t        display: flex;\n672\t        align-items: center;\n673\t        justify-content: center;\n674\t        padding: 24px;\n675\t        font:\n676\t          18px system-ui,\n677\t          sans-serif;\n678\t        color: #c9ccd4;\n679\t        text-align: center;\n680\t        white-space: pre-line;\n681\t        pointer-events: none;\n682\t      }\n683\t      #msg[hidden] {\n684\t        display: none;\n685\t      }\n686\t    </style>\n687\t    <script>\n688\t      window.__engine = { ready: false, errors: [] };\n689\t      function gameFailed(message) {\n690\t        if (!window.__engine || typeof window.__engine.help === 'function') return; // the engine reports now\n691\t        var fix = 'the console names the cause; node x shot labs/game reports it headless';\n692\t        var record = { code: 'APP_START', message: 'the page failed to start: ' + message, fix: fix };\n693\t        if (window.__engine.errors.length === 0) {\n694\t          var msg = document.getElementById('msg');\n695\t          if (msg) msg.textContent = record.code + ': ' + record.message + '\\nFix: ' + fix;\n696\t        }\n697\t        window.__engine.errors.push(record);\n698\t      }\n699\t      addEventListener('error', function (event) {\n700\t        gameFailed(event.message || 'a script failed');\n701\t      });\n702\t      addEventListener('unhandledrejection', function (event) {\n703\t        gameFailed(String((event.reason && event.reason.message) || event.reason));\n704\t      });\n705\t    </script>\n706\t  </head>\n707\t  <body>\n708\t    <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas>\n709\t    <div id=\"msg\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">Loading…</div>\n710\t    <script type=\"module\" src=\"./main.ts\" onerror=\"gameFailed('labs/game/main.ts did not load')\"></script>\n711\t  </body>\n712\t</html>\n713\t/**\n714\t * @file The starter game's scene `game:play`: every new game begins as this one (tools/game/new-game.mjs copies\n715\t * labs/game/ into it), so it is small, plays, and shows the parts a game is made of. The player, a humanoid on the\n716\t * character controller, runs round the arena (`ARENA_TEXT` below: walls, two pillars, two low walls to jump) collecting\n717\t * the gems the seed scatters, before the clock runs out. All the gems: won; the clock at 0: lost.\n718\t *\n719\t * The parts: a level as text, compiled for its physics bodies; settings (`game.*`, read when the scene starts, so they\n720\t * change play and go in replays); components (`gem`, drawn as a small glowing box because it declares `x` and `z` in\n721\t * metres; `round`, the score and the clock); the player (labs/game/cast/player.ts, an actor that reads the step's\n722\t * intents: `move`, `jump`, `dash`); systems in the phase `rules`, after the actors' feet are read back; a named random\n723\t * stream (`gems`); events (`gem`, `won`, `lost`) that pages, sounds and tests hear. The page is labs/game/main.ts, the\n724\t * bot labs/game/bot.ts.\n725\t *\n726\t * The level's text lives here, not in a .txt file, since sim-side code reads no files; the page registers the same\n727\t * text for the view (labs/game/main.ts).\n728\t *\n729\t * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts, so `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` runs it in Node.\n730\t *\n731\t * @example\n732\t * // node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --steps 600\n733\t * // node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --set game.gems=3 --seed 7\n734\t * ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero')?.position; // [3.5, 0, 2.5]\n735\t * @see labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts\n736\t */\n737\timport {\n738\t  addLevelBodies,\n739\t  compileLevel,\n740\t  defineComponent,\n741\t  defineScene,\n742\t  defineSettings,\n743\t  physicsOf,\n744\t  type ActorComponent,\n745\t  type World,\n746\t} from '../../../engine/sim-api';\n747\timport { spawnPlayer } from '../cast/player';\n748\t\n749\t/** The arena: 18 × 14 m inside walls 3 m high, two 2 × 2 m pillars, two low walls (0.75 m) to jump, the spawn `@`. */\n750\texport const ARENA_TEXT = `---\n751\tdescription: The starter game's arena. 18 × 14 m inside walls 3 m high, two 2 × 2 m pillars, two low walls 0.75 m high that the player jumps, and the player's spawn.\n752\t---\n753\t##################\n754\t#................#\n755\t#..@.............#\n756\t#.......PP.......#\n757\t#.......PP.......#\n758\t#................#\n759\t#....w......w....#\n760\t#....w......w....#\n761\t#................#\n762\t#.......PP.......#\n763\t#.......PP.......#\n764\t#................#\n765\t#................#\n766\t##################\n767\t`;\n768\t\n769\t/** The arena, compiled: its colliders for physics, its tiles for the gems and the bot's paths. */\n770\texport const ARENA = compileLevel(ARENA_TEXT, { file: 'labs/game/scenes/play.ts', name: 'arena' });\n771\t\n772\t/** The game's settings: each is reachable through `x set`, URL parameters (`?game.gems=3`) and `__engine.set`. */\n773\texport const GAME_SETTINGS = defineSettings({\n774\t  'game.gems': {\n775\t    type: 'integer',\n776\t    default: 10,\n777\t    minimum: 1,\n778\t    maximum: 40,\n779\t    when: 'scene',\n780\t    description: 'How many gems the seed scatters.',\n781\t  },\n782\t  'game.seconds': {\n783\t    type: 'number',\n784\t    default: 30,\n785\t    minimum: 5,\n786\t    maximum: 600,\n787\t    unit: 's',\n788\t    when: 'scene',\n789\t    description: 'How long the player has to collect them all.',\n790\t  },\n791\t  'game.speed': {\n792\t    type: 'number',\n793\t    default: 6,\n794\t    minimum: 1,\n795\t    maximum: 20,\n796\t    unit: 'm/s',\n797\t    when: 'scene',\n798\t    description: 'How fast the player runs.',\n799\t  },\n800\t});\n801\t\n802\t/** A gem: where it floats and how it looks (the page draws a component that declares x and z in metres). */\n803\texport const GEM = defineComponent('gem', {\n804\t  description: 'A gem to collect: where it floats, its spin and how it is drawn.',\n805\t  fields: {\n806\t    x: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'East.' },\n807\t    y: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, unit: 'm', description: 'Up: the bottom of its box.' },\n808\t    z: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'South.' },\n809\t    yaw: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'rad', description: 'Its spin.' },\n810\t    size: { type: 'number', default: 0.4, unit: 'm', description: 'The side of its box.' },\n811\t    material: { type: 'string', default: 'material:glow', description: 'What it is drawn with.' },\n812\t  },\n813\t});\n814\t\n815\t/** The round: the score, the clock and how it ended. One entity holds it. */\n816\texport const ROUND = defineComponent('round', {\n817\t  description:\n818\t    'The round: gems collected of the total, the steps left on the clock, and whether it is playing, won or lost.',\n819\t  fields: {\n820\t    gems: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many gems the round scattered.' },\n821\t    collected: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many the player has collected.' },\n822\t    left: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'The sim steps left on the clock (60 a second).' },\n823\t    state: { type: 'string', default: 'playing', description: \"'playing', 'won' or 'lost'.\" },\n824\t  },\n825\t});\n826\t\n827\t/** The round, as the component holds it. */\n828\texport type Round = { gems: number; collected: number; left: number; state: 'playing' | 'won' | 'lost' };\n829\t\n830\t/** The scene's components, typed. */\n831\texport type GameComponents = {\n832\t  gem: { x: number; y: number; z: number; yaw: number; size: number; material: string };\n833\t  round: Round;\n834\t  actor: ActorComponent;\n835\t};\n836\t\n837\t/** How close the player's feet come to a gem to collect it, m (on the ground plane). */\n838\texport const REACH = 0.8;\n839\t\n840\t/** How far from the spawn the gems keep, m, so the first one is never collected without moving. */\n841\tconst CLEAR = 3;\n842\t\n843\t/** Where the player starts: the level's spawn `@`. */\n844\tconst START = ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero')!.position;\n845\t\n846\t/** The floor tiles a gem may float over: open floor (`.`), at least `CLEAR` metres from the spawn, in reading order. */\n847\tfunction gemTiles(): number[] {\n848\t  const tiles: number[] = [];\n849\t  ARENA.tiles.forEach((char, i) => {\n850\t    const x = ((i % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n851\t    const z = (Math.floor(i / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n852\t    if (char === '.' && Math.hypot(x - START[0], z - START[2]) >= CLEAR) tiles.push(i);\n853\t  });\n854\t  return tiles;\n855\t}\n856\t\n857\t/** Scatters `count` gems over distinct floor tiles drawn from the stream `gems`: each seed lays them its own way. */\n858\tfunction scatterGems(w: World<GameComponents>, count: number): void {\n859\t  const tiles = gemTiles();\n860\t  const rng = w.rng('gems');\n861\t  for (let k = 0; k < count && tiles.length > 0; k++) {\n862\t    const [tile] = tiles.splice(rng.int(0, tiles.length - 1), 1);\n863\t    const x = ((tile % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n864\t    const z = (Math.floor(tile / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n865\t    w.spawn({ gem: { x, z, yaw: rng.range(0, Math.PI) } });\n866\t  }\n867\t}\n868\t\n869\t/** Ends the round: the player stops reading intents (and so stands still), and the event says how it ended. */\n870\tfunction endRound(w: World<GameComponents>, round: Round, state: 'won' | 'lost'): void {\n871\t  round.state = state;\n872\t  for (const e of w.query('actor')) {\n873\t    e.actor.player = false;\n874\t    e.actor.intents = {};\n875\t  }\n876\t  w.emit(state, { collected: round.collected, gems: round.gems, left: round.left });\n877\t}\n878\t\n879\t/**\n880\t * Spins the gems, collects those an actor's feet reach, and ends the round when the last one goes. A despawn waits for\n881\t * the end of the step, so a gem taken this step is remembered: two actors never count it twice.\n882\t */\n883\tfunction collect(w: World<GameComponents>): void {\n884\t  const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n885\t  for (const gem of w.query('gem')) gem.gem.yaw += 2 * w.dt;\n886\t  if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n887\t  const taken = new Set<number>();\n888\t  for (const { actor } of w.query('actor')) {\n889\t    for (const gem of w.query('gem')) {\n890\t      if (taken.has(gem.id) || Math.hypot(gem.gem.x - actor.x, gem.gem.z - actor.z) > REACH) continue;\n891\t      taken.add(gem.id);\n892\t      w.despawn(gem.id);\n893\t      round.collected += 1;\n894\t      w.emit('gem', { id: gem.id, x: gem.gem.x, z: gem.gem.z, collected: round.collected });\n895\t    }\n896\t  }\n897\t  if (round.collected >= round.gems) endRound(w, round, 'won');\n898\t}\n899\t\n900\t/** Runs the clock down a step at a time (game time is whole steps, never summed seconds); at 0 the round is lost. */\n901\tfunction clock(w: World<GameComponents>): void {\n902\t  const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n903\t  if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n904\t  round.left = Math.max(0, round.left - 1);\n905\t  if (round.left === 0) endRound(w, round, 'lost');\n906\t}\n907\t\n908\t/** The starter game: collect every gem before the clock runs out. */\n909\texport const play = defineScene<GameComponents>('game:play', {\n910\t  description: 'The starter game: run round the arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out.',\n911\t  level: 'level:arena',\n912\t  physics: true,\n913\t  setup(w) {\n914\t    addLevelBodies(physicsOf(w), ARENA);\n915\t    const gems = w.settings.get<number>('game.gems');\n916\t    w.spawn({ round: { gems, left: Math.round(w.settings.get<number>('game.seconds') * w.hz) } });\n917\t    scatterGems(w, gems);\n918\t    spawnPlayer(w, [START[0], START[1], START[2]], w.settings.get<number>('game.speed'));\n919\t    w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' });\n920\t    w.systems.add('game.clock', clock, { phase: 'rules' });\n921\t  },\n922\t});\n923\t\n924\texport default play;\n925\t/**\n926\t * @file The starter game's player: the character the keys move (the character controller's numbers: how it runs,\n927\t * jumps and dashes) and the body it is drawn as, and `spawnPlayer`, which stands it in a scene. A game keeps its\n928\t * cast here, one character a file, as the box keeps its hero, walkers, slimes and wisps (labs/box/cast/); its scenes\n929\t * spawn them. `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --feel` measures how the player handles: response, top speed, the\n930\t * jump's height and air time, the dash's reach.\n931\t *\n932\t * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts.\n933\t *\n934\t * @example\n935\t * playerCharacter(6).speed; // 6: m/s\n936\t * playerCharacter(6).jumpSpeed; // 9: m/s at take-off, about 1.27 m up, over the arena's 0.75 m low walls\n937\t * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\n938\t */\n939\timport { createCharacter, spawnActor, type Character, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\n940\t\n941\t/** The player's take-off speed, m/s: about 1.27 m up under the controller's gravity, over a 0.75 m low wall. */\n942\texport const JUMP_SPEED = 9;\n943\t\n944\t/** The body the player is drawn as: the humanoid in the chibi build, empty-handed. */\n945\texport const PLAYER_BODY = Object.freeze({ build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' });\n946\t\n947\t/** The player's character at `speed` m/s; createCharacter's defaults for the rest (its dash among them). */\n948\texport function playerCharacter(speed: number): Character {\n949\t  return createCharacter({ speed, jumpSpeed: JUMP_SPEED });\n950\t}\n951\t\n952\t/** Stands the player in `w` (a scene with physics) at `at`, facing `yaw`: an actor the step's intents move. */\n953\texport function spawnPlayer<C extends object>(\n954\t  w: World<C>,\n955\t  at: readonly [number, number, number],\n956\t  speed: number,\n957\t  yaw = 0,\n958\t): number {\n959\t  const character = playerCharacter(speed);\n960\t  return spawnActor(w, { at: [at[0], at[1], at[2]], character, player: true, yaw, anim: { ...PLAYER_BODY } });\n961\t}\n962\t/**\n963\t * @file The starter game's bot: game code that plays `game:play` (labs/game/scenes/play.ts) through intents, the way\n964\t * a player's keys do, so a test or an agent plays the game with nobody at the keys. Each step it reads what a player\n965\t * would see (`viewOf`: the player's feet and the gems left) and answers with the step's intents (`botIntents`: run\n966\t * down the arena's flow field to the nearest gem, round the pillars and the low walls).\n967\t *\n968\t * The bot never reaches into the sim: it reads the engine's inspector (`state`) and writes intents through `step`, so\n969\t * what it plays is recorded like a person's play, and `input.record()` turns it into a replay\n970\t * (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json). A game keeps its bot as the game grows: the tests ask it to win\n971\t * (labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts), which proves the game can be won, on every seed they try.\n972\t *\n973\t * Game code, page-side: it imports only engine/index.ts and the game's own modules.\n974\t *\n975\t * @example\n976\t * // import { createHeadless } from '../../engine';\n977\t * // const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play, seed: 3 });\n978\t * // const end = playBot(engine); // { state: 'won', steps: 1234 }\n979\t * botIntents({ player: { x: 3.5, z: 2.5 }, gems: [{ x: 3.5, z: 9.5 }] }).move; // [0, 1]: south, toward the gem\n980\t * @see labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts\n981\t */\n982\timport { NavGrid, type Headless, type Intents } from '../../engine';\n983\timport { ARENA, type Round } from './scenes/play';\n984\t\n985\t/** A point on the ground, m: x east, z south. */\n986\texport interface GroundSpot {\n987\t  x: number;\n988\t  z: number;\n989\t}\n990\t\n991\t/** What the bot sees each step: the player's feet and the gems left. */\n992\texport interface BotView {\n993\t  player: GroundSpot;\n994\t  gems: readonly GroundSpot[];\n995\t}\n996\t\n997\t/** The arena's walkable grid: the flow fields the bot follows go round its pillars and low walls. */\n998\tconst GRID = new NavGrid(ARENA);\n999\t\n1000\t/** How many headings the bot steers by: few, so its intents change seldom and its replays stay short. */\n1001\texport const HEADINGS = 16;\n1002\t\n1003\t/** The intents for one step: run toward the nearest gem by walking distance; none when no gem is left. */\n1004\texport function botIntents(view: BotView): Intents {\n1005\t  if (view.gems.length === 0) return {};\n1006\t  const goals = [...new Set(view.gems.map((gem) => GRID.nearestOpen(gem.x, gem.z)))].filter((tile) => tile >= 0);\n1007\t  const way = goals.length > 0 ? GRID.flowField(goals).direction(view.player.x, view.player.z) : null;\n1008\t  if (way) return { move: heading(way.x, way.z) };\n1009\t  // On a gem's tile (or nowhere the field leads): straight at the nearest gem.\n1010\t  const near = [...view.gems].sort((a, b) => distance(view.player, a) - distance(view.player, b))[0];\n1011\t  const d = distance(view.player, near);\n1012\t  return d < 1e-6 ? {} : { move: heading(near.x - view.player.x, near.z - view.player.z) };\n1013\t}\n1014\t\n1015\t/** The nearest of the `HEADINGS` directions to (x, z), as `move` takes it: two decimals cut toward 0, never past 1. */\n1016\tfunction heading(x: number, z: number): [number, number] {\n1017\t  const step = (2 * Math.PI) / HEADINGS;\n1018\t  const angle = Math.round(Math.atan2(x, z) / step) * step;\n1019\t  return [Math.trunc(Math.sin(angle) * 100) / 100 || 0, Math.trunc(Math.cos(angle) * 100) / 100 || 0];\n1020\t}\n1021\t\n1022\t/** The distance between two spots on the ground, m. */\n1023\tfunction distance(a: GroundSpot, b: GroundSpot): number {\n1024\t  return Math.hypot(b.x - a.x, b.z - a.z);\n1025\t}\n1026\t\n1027\t/** What the bot sees in a running engine: the player's feet and the gems left, read through the inspector. */\n1028\texport function viewOf(engine: Headless): BotView {\n1029\t  const [player] = engine.state('actor').entities as unknown as { actor: GroundSpot }[];\n1030\t  const gems = (engine.state('gem').entities as unknown as { gem: GroundSpot }[]).map(({ gem }) => ({\n1031\t    x: gem.x,\n1032\t    z: gem.z,\n1033\t  }));\n1034\t  return { player: { x: player.actor.x, z: player.actor.z }, gems };\n1035\t}\n1036\t\n1037\t/** The round as it stands, read through the inspector. */\n1038\texport function roundOf(engine: Headless): Round {\n1039\t  const [entity] = engine.state('round').entities as unknown as { round: Round }[];\n1040\t  return entity.round;\n1041\t}\n1042\t\n1043\t/** Plays the bot until the round ends or `limit` steps pass; returns how the round stands and the steps it took. */\n1044\texport function playBot(engine: Headless, limit = 60 * 600): { state: Round['state']; steps: number } {\n1045\t  let steps = 0;\n1046\t  while (roundOf(engine).state === 'playing' && steps < limit) {\n1047\t    engine.step(1, botIntents(viewOf(engine)));\n1048\t    steps += 1;\n1049\t  }\n1050\t  return { state: roundOf(engine).state, steps };\n1051\t}\n1052\t/**\n1053\t * @file The starter game's tests (labs/game/scenes/play.ts): what every game made from it starts with, and keeps as it\n1054\t * grows. They run headless in Node (`npm test`), in a few seconds:\n1055\t * - its golden replay (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json, the bot's play) gives its golden hashes, as `x replay`\n1056\t *   checks it: on another platform than the one that recorded them, two runs agree instead;\n1057\t * - a run continues exactly after a capture and a restore;\n1058\t * - the bot (labs/game/bot.ts) wins on 10 seeds, so the game can be won, however the gems fall;\n1059\t * - the clock runs out on a player who stands still, and the settings change play;\n1060\t * - two actors that reach a gem in the same step take it once.\n1061\t *\n1062\t * When the game's play changes on purpose, record the goldens again: `node x replay labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json\n1063\t * --browser sim --update` (in Node and Chromium), and say why in the commit.\n1064\t * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\n1065\t */\n1066\timport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n1067\timport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\n1068\timport { createHeadless } from '../../../engine';\n1069\timport { defineScene, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\n1070\timport { botIntents, playBot, roundOf, viewOf } from '../bot';\n1071\timport { spawnPlayer } from '../cast/player';\n1072\timport { play, type GameComponents } from './play';\n1073\t\n1074\t/** The golden replay: the bot's play from seed 1, with the goldens `x replay --update` recorded. */\n1075\tconst REPLAY = readFileSync(new URL('../replays/bot.replay.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\n1076\t\n1077\t/** This platform's golden key, as replays write it. */\n1078\tconst PLATFORM = `${process.platform}-${process.arch}`;\n1079\t\n1080\t/** Plays `text` from step 0 in a fresh engine and returns its hashes at the replay's checkpoints. */\n1081\tasync function replayed(text: string): Promise<Record<string, string>> {\n1082\t  const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play });\n1083\t  const result = await (engine.replay as (text: string) => Promise<{ hashes: Record<string, string> }>)(text);\n1084\t  return result.hashes;\n1085\t}\n1086\t\n1087\tdescribe('game:play', () => {\n1088\t  it('replays the bot’s play to its golden hashes', async () => {\n1089\t    const goldens = (JSON.parse(REPLAY) as { hashes: Record<string, Record<string, string>> }).hashes;\n1090\t    const first = await replayed(REPLAY);\n1091\t    if (goldens[PLATFORM]) expect(first).toEqual(goldens[PLATFORM]);\n1092\t    else expect(await replayed(REPLAY)).toEqual(first); // recorded elsewhere: the runs agree, as x replay judges it\n1093\t  });\n1094\t\n1095\t  it('continues exactly after a capture and a restore', async () => {\n1096\t    const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play, seed: 4 });\n1097\t    for (let k = 0; k < 120; k++) engine.step(1, botIntents(viewOf(engine)));\n1098\t    const capture = engine.capture();\n1099\t    for (let k = 0; k < 120; k++) engine.step(1, botIntents(viewOf(engine)));\n1100\t    const straight = engine.hash();\n1101\t    engine.restore(capture);\n1102\t    for (let k = 0; k < 120; k++) engine.step(1, botIntents(viewOf(engine)));\n1103\t    expect(engine.hash()).toBe(straight);\n1104\t  });\n1105\t\n1106\t  it('is won by the bot on 10 seeds', async () => {\n1107\t    for (let seed = 1; seed <= 10; seed++) {\n1108\t      const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play, seed });\n1109\t      expect({ seed, ...playBot(engine) }).toMatchObject({ seed, state: 'won' });\n1110\t      expect(roundOf(engine).collected).toBe(roundOf(engine).gems);\n1111\t    }\n1112\t  });\n1113\t\n1114\t  it('is lost when the clock runs out, and its settings change play', async () => {\n1115\t    const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play, settings: { 'game.seconds': 5, 'game.gems': 3 } });\n1116\t    engine.step(5 * 60);\n1117\t    expect(roundOf(engine)).toMatchObject({ gems: 3, collected: 0, left: 0, state: 'lost' });\n1118\t    expect(viewOf(engine).gems).toHaveLength(3);\n1119\t  });\n1120\t\n1121\t  it('counts a gem once when two actors reach it in the same step', async () => {\n1122\t    const twin = defineScene<GameComponents>('game:twin', {\n1123\t      description: 'The starter, and two more players put on its gem in the first step: both reach it in the next.',\n1124\t      level: 'level:arena',\n1125\t      physics: true,\n1126\t      setup(w) {\n1127\t        play.setup(w);\n1128\t        const twins = (world: World<GameComponents>) => {\n1129\t          if ([...world.query('actor')].length > 1) return;\n1130\t          const [{ gem }] = world.query('gem');\n1131\t          for (const dx of [-0.3, 0.3]) spawnPlayer(world, [gem.x + dx, 0, gem.z], 6);\n1132\t        };\n1133\t        w.systems.add('test.twins', twins, { phase: 'rules' });\n1134\t      },\n1135\t    });\n1136\t    const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: twin, settings: { 'game.gems': 1 } }); // no neighbour within reach\n1137\t    engine.step(2);\n1138\t    expect([roundOf(engine).collected, roundOf(engine).state, engine.state('gem').entities.length]).toEqual([1, 'won', 0]); // prettier-ignore\n1139\t  });\n1140\t});\n1141\t# Engine feedback: weetare\n1142\t\n1143\tWhat this game needed from the my-3dge engine (at 2dbd829) and did not get. The owner collects this file\n1144\tfrom every game to improve the engine, so write each item for an engine agent who has never seen this repository:\n1145\twhat you tried, what happened (the command, the error code), what you expected, and what you did instead.\n1146\t\n1147\t## Requests\n1148\t\n1149\t<!-- What the engine should add or fix, most costly first. -->\n1150\t\n1151\t## Friction\n1152\t\n1153\t<!-- Docs, errors, templates or APIs that slowed you down or misled you, most costly first. -->\n1154\t\n1155\t## Local engine patches\n1156\t\n1157\t<!--\n1158\t`npm run check` accepts an edited engine file only when it is listed here, one line each, its path in backticks:\n1159\t- `engine/path/to/file.ts`: why no workaround in the game would do, and what the engine should change instead.\n1160\t-->\n1161\tlabs/box/README.md\n1162\tlabs/box/clipFiles.ts\n1163\tlabs/box/index.html\n1164\tlabs/box/library.ts\n1165\tlabs/box/main.ts\n1166\tlabs/box/menu.ts\n1167\tlabs/box/menuControls.ts\n1168\tlabs/box/touch.ts\n1169\t\n1170\tlabs/box:\n1171\tREADME.md\n1172\tcast\n1173\tclipFiles.ts\n1174\tindex.html\n1175\tlevels\n1176\tlibrary.ts\n1177\tlooks\n1178\tmain.ts\n1179\tmenu.ts\n1180\tmenuControls.ts\n1181\tscenes\n1182\tsounds\n1183\ttouch.ts\n1184\t\n1185\tlabs/box/cast:\n1186\thero.ts\n1187\tslime.ts\n1188\twalker.ts\n1189\twisp.ts\n1190\t\n1191\tlabs/box/levels:\n1192\thall.txt\n1193\troom.txt\n1194\t\n1195\tlabs/box/looks:\n1196\tdaylight.ts\n1197\thall-floor.ts\n1198\ttorch-bloom.ts\n1199\ttorch-cel.ts\n1200\ttorch-fxaa.ts\n1201\ttorch-lit.ts\n1202\ttorch-pixel.ts\n1203\ttorch-toon.ts\n1204\t\n1205\tlabs/box/scenes:\n1206\tclips.ts\n1207\tcrowd-100.ts\n1208\tcrowd-1000.ts\n1209\tcrowd-5000.ts\n1210\tfight-1000.ts\n1211\tfight-foes.ts\n1212\tfight-hero.ts\n1213\tfight-rules.ts\n1214\tfight.ts\n1215\thall-props.ts\n1216\thall.ts\n1217\tragdoll.ts\n1218\troom-hero.ts\n1219\troom.ts\n1220\t\n1221\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae","uuid":"360865d8-218a-4c69-a43e-a96c303ccc9a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:44:14.261Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-024328--x54facc-work/9d46f5b4-97b7-46a5-8fb8-5eac466a6eae/tool-results/bsy4z2ug0.txt","content":"# AGENTS.md: weetare\n\nweetare is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\nWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\nis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n\nBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\nit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\nthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n\n## Set up (once per machine)\n\n    bash scripts/setup.sh\n\nIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n   labs/game/main.ts.\n2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n\n`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n  configs and the docs it shipped: engine-lock.json lists every one, and `npm run check` fails when one changes. When\n  the engine is in your way, work round it in the game and write what you needed in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. Only when no\n  workaround exists, patch the engine file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" there, with why.\n- **Only the public API.** Pages import engine/index.ts; sim-side code imports engine/sim-api.ts (lint enforces it).\n- **Reproducible play.** Sim-side code reads no clock, no `Math.random`, no DOM: randomness comes from named streams\n  (`w.rng('loot')`), time is whole 60 Hz steps. The same intents and seed give the same hashes, in Node and Chromium.\n- **Assets are code or text first**: geometry, textures, materials and sounds as data. A binary file only with the\n  owner's approval, listed in APPROVED-BINARIES.json with their answer (`npm run check` fails one unlisted); fonts are\n  approved, anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them.\n- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines.\n- **Ask the owner** before anything outward or costly: a binary asset, a new npm dependency, spending money,\n  publishing. No answer within 15 minutes: take the safer choice, note it in labs/game/README.md, carry on.\n- Update the engine only when the owner asks: `node x engine --update`.\n\n## Done means\n\n`npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the bot still wins (teach it each new rule); a change that alters play on\npurpose records the replay's goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) with the reason\nin the commit; labs/game/README.md says what the game is now.\n# Making a game with my-3dge\n\nYou are making a game with the my-3dge engine. Read this once, start to finish; after that, look things up (section\n8). A game's AGENTS.md sends you here after `node tools/game/new-game.mjs` made the game. For a model without the\nrepository, this one file is also the engine in brief: its public API by area is section 10.\n\n## 1. What you have\n\nmy-3dge is a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier physics, the\nUI in HTML/CSS. Three promises shape everything you write:\n\n- **Play is reproducible.** The rules (the _sim_) run on the CPU at a fixed 60 steps a second. The same seed and the\n  same intents give the same state, bit for bit, in Node and in Chromium; every state has a 16-digit hash.\n- **Nothing needs a screen.** The sim runs headless in Node (tests, `node x sim`). The page renders on WebGPU in a\n  headless Chromium (`node x shot`) and reports what it drew as numbers: which object covers which pixels (the ID\n  pass), the look's metrics, a text thumbnail. You prove a game works with commands, not by looking.\n- **Code and data, not binary files.** Levels are text maps; materials and textures are generators with parameters;\n  sounds are voices and tracker notes; characters are procedural bodies, with a library of readable motion clips.\n\nA game's repository:\n\n| Path                                                             | What it is                                                            |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `labs/game/`                                                     | The game: the page, the rules (`scenes/`), the characters (`cast/`), the bot, the replays |\n| `labs/box/`                                                      | The engine's worked example: a hero, crowds, combat, props, looks, sound (read-only)   |\n| `engine/`                                                        | The engine. Game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts            |\n| `tools/`, `x.js`                                                 | The `node x` commands, the lint rules, the templates (tools/templates/)               |\n| `data/anim/`                                                     | The animation library: readable clips, and the engine's actions                       |\n| `docs/`                                                          | This guide, API.md (every public name) and ERRORS.md (every error code, with its fix) |\n| `engine-lock.json`, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`                         | The engine's pinned commit and file hashes; what you needed from the engine           |\n\nEverything but `labs/game/` and the root's game files (AGENTS.md, README.md, package.json…) is the engine's,\nread-only: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json.\n\n## 2. The model\n\n**Two halves.** The sim is the game's rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them each\nstep, events. Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts and never touches the\nDOM, the GPU, the clock or `Math.random`. The page (labs/game/main.ts) imports engine/index.ts: `createEngine` starts\nthe sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into intents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame`\nfor the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The page reads the sim and never writes it. Lint enforces the split.\n\n**A scene** is the unit `x sim`, the page and the tests run: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings,\nsetup(w), step(w, intents) })`. `setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. One page can\noffer several scenes (`createEngine({ scenes })`, `?scene=<id>` picks one); a running page cannot switch scenes yet,\nso a level select reloads the page with another `?scene`.\n\n**The world, `w`**, is what systems get:\n\n- **Components and entities.** `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields\n  with defaults and units, so the state can be hashed, captured and checked. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an\n  id; `w.query('gem')` returns the live entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)`, `w.add`, `w.remove` and\n  `w.despawn` do the rest. An entity whose component declares `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box until\n  something draws it properly (an actor, a prop, a crowd body).\n- **Systems** run in phases, in this order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`.\n  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` puts a rule after the physics moved the bodies and the\n  readback copied their places into components.\n- **Events.** `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)` listeners. A page\n  reads them from `frame.world.events.trace()` (sounds, effects); emitters burst on them; tests count them.\n- **Time** is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800), never by\n  adding up `w.dt`: floating-point sums drift. `w.timers` schedule in sim seconds.\n- **Randomness** comes from named streams seeded by the run: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n- **Settings** are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum,\n  unit, when: 'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`. Each is reachable from the URL\n  (`?game.gems=20`), from `node x sim … --set game.gems=20`, `node x set` and `__engine.set`, and replays record them.\n\n**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once per step, as plain data: `move` (a world-space\ndirection `[x, z]`), `b` (buttons held), `p` (pressed this step), `aim`, `look`, `cam`, and the game's own\n`'game:<name>'` keys. A player actor reads the step's intents itself; systems get them as their second argument\n(`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). In a page, devices make intents through an action map\n(`actionMap:thirdPerson` by default: WASD or the arrows run, Space jumps, Shift dashes); a map with `look: true` sends\nthe camera's view as `look`, and its `aimWith` fills `aim` (`centre`: what the screen's middle points at; `pointer`:\nthe pointer on the player's floor), and a game with several camera modes gives `createEngine` a map per camera\n(`actionMap: (code) => …`). A bot or a test writes them directly: `engine.step(1, { move: [1, 0] })`. A replay is the\nintents of a run, recorded.\n\n**Proof.** `engine.hash()` digests the whole sim. A replay file (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json) holds a scene,\nseed, settings, the intents at each step that changed them, and the golden hashes at its checkpoints, per platform\n(`linux-x64`). `capture()` and `restore()` copy a run and continue it exactly. If a hash changes, play changed.\n\n## 3. The starter, file by file\n\nThe starter (labs/game/) is a whole small game: run round an arena collecting every gem before the clock runs out.\nGrow your game from it; keep its tests as they are, and change what they check as the game changes.\n\n| File                         | What it shows                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.ts     | The rules: a level as text, settings, components (`gem`, `round`), systems, a random stream, events |\n| labs/game/cast/player.ts     | The player: a character on the controller (run, jump, dash) drawn as the humanoid          |\n| labs/game/bot.ts             | A bot that plays through intents, steering down a flow field to the nearest gem            |\n| labs/game/main.ts            | The page: `createEngine`, the HUD in `onFrame`, Enter to play again, Esc's pause menu, the chase camera     |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts | The tests: the golden replay, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds, the clock     |\n| labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json | The bot's play, recorded, with its golden hashes                                      |\n| labs/game/README.md          | The game's own page: what it is, how it plays, the calls made                              |\n\n## 4. What to reach for\n\nThe public names below are in engine/sim-api.ts (sim-side) or engine/index.ts (pages); section 10 lists them all.\nThe box (labs/box/, its README says which scene shows what) has a working use of almost each one.\n\n| You need                       | Reach for                                                                                 | See it in                          |\n| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| A level                        | A text map in a scene module; `compileLevel`, glyphs (`#` wall, `.` floor, `@` spawn, `w` low wall, `P` pillar, `^` stairs, `=` gallery, `o` dais, `t` brazier), `defineGlyph` for your own; `addLevelBodies`; the page registers the text (`createEngine({ levels })`); a building of storeys: `node x new level`; buildings on a terrain: `defineSite`, `addSiteBodies` | labs/game/scenes/play.ts, labs/box/levels/hall.txt, labs/playground/levels/site.ts |\n| A player or character          | `createCharacter` (speed, jump, dash, coyote time, buffer), `spawnActor`, `measureFeel`; Apex's movement, `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`, or your own: `defineMovement` (`node x new movement`); `node x feel`, `createFeelPanel` | labs/game/cast/player.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts, labs/playground/cast/player.ts |\n| Physics                        | `physics: true` in the scene, `physicsOf(w)`: bodies, `raycast`, `overlap`, `probe`; `addBox`, `addSensor`, `knock`, movers | labs/box/scenes/hall.ts |\n| Props                          | `defineProp` (meshes, a body, hooks), `spawnProp`, `hitProp`; the engine's `prop:crate`, `prop:barrel`, `prop:brazier`, `prop:pillar`; one a player uses or strikes: `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `addInteraction` (`node x new interactable`) | labs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts |\n| Rides, doors and switches      | `addLevelLadders`, `addZipline`, `addLauncher`; `addLevelDoors`, `addLevelWindows`, `addSwitch`, `switchGroup`; `setDaytime`, `lightsAtDusk`; `placeActor` | labs/playground/scenes/site.ts |\n| Enemies and crowds             | Crowd bodies (`addAgent`, `updateCrowd`, `knockback`, `killAgent`), steering (`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`), `NavGrid` and `FlowField`, attack tokens, `canSee` | labs/box/cast/walker.ts, labs/box/scenes/crowd-1000.ts |\n| Combat                         | Moves as data (`defineMove`, `Attack`, `Combo`), hit queries (`coneHits`, `sweepHits`), `damage` and `HEALTH`, projectiles, telegraphs, `reactTo`, `requestHitStop` | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts |\n| Melee, guns and targets        | `addMelee` (punches, kicks, the door kick), `defineWeapon`, `weaponsFor` and `addWeapons` (hip fire, the sights, a climbing recoil, reloads; `node x new weapon`), `defineThing` and `spawnThing` (targets that fall at a hit and stand again; `node x new thing`) | labs/playground/weapons/, labs/playground/cast/dummies.ts |\n| Ragdolls and joints            | `addJoint` (fixed, ball, hinge, slider, rope, spring, as data), `addRagdolls` and `handOff` (a hard blow or a death gives a person's body to physics; it gets up the way it lies), `standUp` | labs/box/scenes/ragdoll.ts, labs/playground/cast/partners.ts |\n| Animation                      | Actors animate themselves (`spawnActor({ anim: { build } })`); builds, bodies, the animator's body states, actions (`defineAction`, played on an actor with `actOn`), clips from the library (`playClip`) | labs/box/scenes/clips.ts, `node x anim find` |\n| How it looks                   | Materials and textures as data (`defineMaterial`, `defineTexture`), looks (`?post.look=look:storybook`, `post.combine`, `post.filters`, `defineLook`; `__engine.look()`), skies, lights | labs/box/looks/torch-lit.ts, `node x describe look` |\n| Effects                        | Emitters as data, bursting on sim events (`defineEmitter`); telegraphs; screen shake      | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts           |\n| UI                             | `engine.ui`: `hud(id, text, { at })`, `label`, `damage`, `crosshair`; screens and menus: `createMenu`, `createEngine({ store, modal })` (rebinds kept, a menu's keys out of play), `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen` (a controls screen); the rest plain HTML/CSS | labs/game/main.ts (its pause), labs/playground/ui/, labs/box/menu.ts |\n| Sound                          | Effects and songs as data (`defineSfx`, `defineSong`), the page's `createAudio`; a scene's `sounds` maps events to sounds | labs/box/sounds/effects.ts, labs/box/sounds/page.ts |\n| Cameras                        | `?cam=iso` (`threequarter`, `topdown`, `overhead`, `brawler`, `side`), `?cam3=chase` (`orbit`, `fly`, `fixed`, `first`, `rail`, `shoulder`, `follow`); `__engine.camera.set(code, { blend: true })`; a map per mode: `createEngine({ actionMap: (code) => … })`; a mode of your own: `defineCamera` (`node x new camera`) | labs/game/main.ts, labs/playground/views.ts |\n| Touch controls                 | `defineTouchLayout`, `createEngine({ touchLayout })` (built, not yet tested on phones)   | labs/box/touch.ts                  |\n| A new entry of a kind          | `node x new <kind> labs/game/<dir>/<name>`: the module and its test from a template (`node x new` lists the kinds, tools/templates/README.md says where each goes; a new scene starts as a copy of play.ts instead) | tools/templates/                    |\n\nSaves, high scores and settings go in the page's store (`createStore`, never read by the sim), as labs/game/best.ts\nkeeps the best round.\n\n## 5. Rules that keep play reproducible\n\n- Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts; pages only engine/index.ts.\n  Never import three.js or Rapier directly: the public API re-exports what a game needs.\n- No clocks, timers, `Math.random`, `crypto`, DOM, storage, Web Audio or Node built-ins in sim-side code, and no\n  dynamic `import()`: lint refuses each, and its message names the fix. No network either: what the sim needs from\n  outside arrives as intents or settings. Write `Math.pow`, not `**`. Use the standard `Math`: the sim swaps in\n  portable ports while it steps, so the results match in Node and Chromium.\n- Time in steps; randomness from named streams; entities in `w.query`'s id order.\n- A sim-side module reads no files: a level's text lives in a string in the scene module.\n- Presentation never writes the sim: cameras, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change freely without changing a hash.\n- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n\n**Assets.** Make them with code and data first: geometry, textures and materials as generators with parameters,\nsounds as voices and tracker notes, levels as text. A binary file (an image, a recorded sound, a model) needs the\nowner's approval: ask, and once they approve, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json at the game's root as\n`{ \"path\", \"reason\" }`, the reason their answer, who and when; `npm run check` fails a binary listed nowhere. Fonts\nare approved: anywhere in labs/game/, each with its license file beside it.\n\n## 6. Seeing it without a screen\n\nEvery command prints its verdict first, about 20 lines at most, and writes `out/<cmd>/<target>/report.json`;\n`node x help <cmd>` gives its manual.\n\n| Command                                                     | What it does                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `npm test`                                                  | The game's tests (Vitest), headless                                              |\n| `npm run check`                                             | Types, lint, format, `node x check` (the asset rules; the engine as pinned)       |\n| `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--steps n] [--seed s] [--set k=v] [--feel]` | Runs a scene in Node: its hash, events, entities; `--feel` measures the player |\n| `node x replay labs/game/replays [--browser sim] [--update] [--bisect]` | Checks replays' goldens; `--update` records them; `--bisect` finds where two runs part |\n| `node x shot labs/game [--cam code] [--set k=v] [--png]`    | Renders the page on WebGPU: coverage, visible objects, a text thumbnail; an image only with `--png` |\n| `node x eye labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--cam code] [--steps n]` | What a camera sees of the bodies, in Node with no GPU: a text picture and its legend (`__engine.eye()` in a page) |\n| `node x film labs/game --steps \"…\" [--compare ref]`         | Films scripted play as a contact sheet; `--compare` names the objects whose pixels changed |\n| `node x gallery <kind> [id] --page labs/game [--look id]`   | Films every prop, material, emitter, look, body or move as a labelled contact sheet; `#gallery/<kind>/<id>` opens one on any page |\n| `node x inspect --page labs/game`, then `node x eval \"<js>\"`, `node x dump`, `node x set <key> <value>` | A live headless page to question: the HUD through DOM queries, `__engine` |\n| `node x perf labs/game [--scene id]`                        | Performance as counters (ms per step and frame, draw calls), never frames per second |\n| `node x describe [kind [id]]`                               | The registries: every kind's fields and entries (materials, moves, looks, sounds…) |\n| `node x qa geo tex audio`                                   | Content QA by numbers: the engine's and the game's geometry, props, textures and sounds (labs/game/'s geometry, props, textures and sounds folders); a new or worse violation fails, and one the game accepts goes in its own `tests/baselines/qa-<family>-game.json` with the reason |\n| `node x new <kind> <id>`                                    | A new entry from its template, with its test                                     |\n| `node x audio sfx:<id>` · `node x anim find <words>`        | A sound rendered in Node, as numbers · the clip library searched                 |\n| `node x engine`                                             | Which engine the game is on, and whether its files are as shipped                |\n\nIn a page, `window.__engine` is the inspector, and a headless engine in tests (`createHeadless({ scene, seed,\nsettings })`) has the same members but the rendering ones: `__engine.help()` lists them (`step`, `hash`,\n`capture`, `restore`, `replay`, `input.press`, `input.record`, `set`, `state`, `shot`,\n`stats`…).\n\n## 7. Testing a game\n\n- The starter's test shows the four kinds every game keeps: its golden replay, a run that continues exactly after a\n  capture and a restore, the bot winning on 10 seeds, and a rule (the clock). Tests sit beside their module\n  (`play.test.ts`) and run with `npm test`.\n- Keep the bot as the game grows: teach it each new rule. \"The bot wins on 10 seeds\" keeps proving the game can be won,\n  and finds the seeds that softlock.\n- Check the UI through the DOM: the overlay's elements are `[data-ui]` (`node x inspect`, then `node x eval`).\n- When play changes on purpose, record the goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) and\n  say why in the commit. When a hash changes by surprise, `node x replay <file> --browser sim --bisect` narrows it\n  down: to the step, entity and field where two runs part (Node and Chromium, or Node and Node again), or, when every\n  run agrees but they all left the goldens, to the checkpoints between which they did (then the code, data or a\n  dependency changed what the sim does). The usual causes: a `Math` function held in a variable, `**`, an iteration\n  whose order is not fixed, module state between runs, view settings or presentation reaching the sim, `async` code in\n  a step.\n\n## 8. Looking things up\n\n- **docs/API.md**: every public name with the first sentence of its doc comment, the public API first. The comment at\n  the link is the full manual: grep engine/ for the name.\n- **docs/ERRORS.md**: every error and advice code with its fix. Errors print as `[CODE] message`, and the fix says\n  what to do.\n- **The engine's file comments**: each module opens with what it is for, its rules and a runnable `@example`.\n- **`node x describe`** for data, **`node x help`** for commands, **`__engine.help()`** for the inspector.\n- **The engine's repository** (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge) for its plan, its decisions and its tests.\n\n## 9. When the engine is in your way\n\n1. Work round it in the game: a component, a system, page code of your own.\n2. Write it in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md for an engine agent who has never seen your game: what you tried, what happened\n   (the command, the error code), what you expected, what you did instead. The owner collects these from every game.\n3. Never change the engine quietly: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json. When no\n   workaround exists, patch the file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, with why.\n4. The owner decides when a game moves to a newer engine (`node x engine --update`). It replaces nothing of the\n   game's own (it stops and names what is in the way); the engine's package.json entries win, and it names any it\n   drops or sets back. It says which root files it brought to the engine's templates, and which ones you edited\n   whose templates changed: compare those with tools/game/template/ and take what applies. After it: `npm ci`,\n   `npm run check`, `npm test`, and record the goldens again where play changed.\n\n## 10. The public API by area\n\n<!-- api-map:start: generated by `node x docs --write` from the area comments of engine/sim-api.ts, engine/anim/api.ts and engine/index.ts; edit those, not this section -->\nSim-side code imports these from engine/sim-api.ts; pages get them from engine/index.ts as well. Each name has the\nfirst sentence of its doc comment in docs/API.md, and its whole manual where it is declared.\n\n**Scenes**: defined as data with a setup and a step, run by x sim, x replay and createHeadless.\n`defineScene`, `defineDevAction`; types `Scene`, `SceneSpec`, `SceneAction`, `SceneSound`, `DevActionSpec`.\n\n**The world**: entities, components, systems, timers, events.\n`defineComponent`, `PHASES`; types `World`, `SimRng`, `WorldTimers`, `WorldEvents`, `Entity`, `With`, `SpawnSpec`, `ComponentOf`, `Phase`, `SystemFn`.\n\n**Registries and settings**: content as data, one schema for every setting.\n`defineKind`, `def`, `defineSettings`; types `Entry`, `Kind`, `KindSpec`, `Field`, `Schema`, `SimSettings`, `SettingValue`.\n\n**Intents**: what the sim reads from outside each step.\n`held`, `pressed`, `fromCamera`, `INTENT_KEYS`, `createInputBuffer`, `updateInputBuffer`, `released`, `buffered`, `consume`, `consumeAll`, `repeated`, `BUFFER_STEPS`, `REPEAT_DELAY`, `REPEAT_RATE`; types `Intents`, `IntentValue`, `CustomIntentKey`, `InputBuffer`.\n\n**Math**: three.js's math classes and the engine's helpers (MathUtils covers clamp, lerp, damp, smoothstep).\n`Vector3`, `Quaternion`, `Matrix4`, `Euler`, `Box3`, `Sphere`, `Ray`, `Plane`, `Color`, `MathUtils`, `approach`, `angDiff`, `lerpAng`, `approachAng`, `smoothDamp`, `ease`, `swingTwist`.\n\n**Randomness**: seeded streams (the world's are w.rng('<stream>')).\n`Rng`.\n\n**Events**: emitted with w.emit, heard with w.events.on.\ntypes `EventMap`, `Listener`, `TraceRecord`.\n\n**Levels**: a legend of glyphs, level text compiled to plain data (heights, solid grid, colliders, meshes, spawns).\n`compileLevel`, `validateLevel`, `defineGlyph`, `defineLevel`, `getLevel`, `floorHeight`, `topHeight`, `solidAt`, `tileAt`, `LEVEL_FORMAT`; types `CompileOptions`, `LevelSource`, `LevelProblem`, `Glyph`, `GlyphSpec`, `LevelEntry`, `CompiledLevel`, `LevelGlyph`, `BoxCollider`, `ColliderDescriptor`, `GroundDescriptor`, `MeshDescriptor`, `SpawnPoint`, `LightSpot`, `TileRect`, `Point3`.\n\n**Animation (WP 3.4)**: the humanoid skeleton (a root plus 23 joints) and its builds, poses with three.js quaternions, blending under bone masks, additive layers, forward kinematics, SVG contact sheets and the pose checks.\n`JOINTS`, `SOCKETS`, `MASKS`, `createSkeleton`, `boneMask`, `defineBuild`, `getBuild`, `Pose`, `blendPoses`, `makeAdditive`, `addPose`, `hashPose`, `forwardKinematics`, `createWorldPose`, `jointPosition`, `jointRotation`, `jointMatrix`, `jointToWorld`, `socketPosition`, `tipPosition`, `animSheet`, `animCheck`, `twoBone`, `twoBoneRot`, `LIMBS`, `aim`, `AIM_CHAIN`, `footPlant`, `createFootPlantState`, `FOOT_LOCK`, `FOOT_PLANT`, `FootPlant`, `queryGround`, `handTo`; types `JointName`, `SocketName`, `MaskName`, `Skeleton`, `BoneMask`, `Build`, `BuildSpec`, `JointRef`, `BlendOptions`, `WorldPose`, `Sheet`, `SheetFrame`, `SheetOptions`, `SheetView`, `AnimCheckSpec`, `AnimCheckReport`, `AnimProblem`, `RigidPair`, `PointName`, `TwoBoneOptions`, `LimbEnd`, `Limb`, `AimOptions`, `AimLink`, `FootPlantOptions`, `FootPlantState`, `FootLock`, `FootPlantLayerOptions`, `GroundQueries`, `GroundProbe`, `GroundHit`.\n\n**The procedural humanoid (WP 3.6)**: my-3d2dge's gait, idle, lean, arm swing, held poses and stances as effector targets written with B(forward, right, up), solved onto the skeleton; a fixed step and a seeded stream.\n`Humanoid`, `POSES`, `STANCES`, `WEAPONS`, `B`, `turnRight`; types `HumanoidOptions`, `HumanoidInput`, `HumanoidState`, `HumanoidWeights`, `BodyMotion`, `PoseName`, `StanceName`, `Weapon`, `Effectors`, `FRU`, `LimbSide`, `Sided`.\n\n**The character animator (WP 3.7)**: a body driven through an explicit layer stack (weights, base, override, solve, then layers in clips, additive, post, physics and secondary), told its body states each step (unknown words warn with the closest), with footsteps, sockets, a trail and detail levels; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n`Animator`, `BODY_STATES`, `checkBodyStates`, `TRAILS`, `SAMPLE_STATES`, `SLOTS`, `LAYER_SLOTS`, `LOD`, `easeWeight`, `HUMANOID_WEIGHTS`; types `AnimatorOptions`, `PoseOptions`, `AnimatorReport`, `AnimEvent`, `TrailSample`, `BodyStateName`, `AnimatorInput`, `TrailName`, `SampleState`, `SlotName`, `LayerSlot`, `AnimLayer`, `WeightRates`, `WeightInfo`.\n\n**Blob and floater rigs (WP 5.3)**: my-3d2dge's squash-and-stretch blob on a minimal skeleton (a volume-preserving squash spring, a smoothed look, wings, its moves as data: bite, leap, pounce, spit) and a floater that hovers over the floor, banks into turns and carries orbiting motes; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n`Blob`, `BLOB`, `BLOB_JOINTS`, `BLOB_LEAP`, `BLOB_MOVES`, `blobMove`, `blobMoveTimes`, `Floater`, `FLOATER`; types `BlobOptions`, `BlobInput`, `BlobState`, `BlobJoint`, `BlobPose`, `BlobLeap`, `BlobMove`, `BlobMoveName`, `BlobMoveFrame`, `FloaterOptions`, `FloaterInput`, `FloaterState`, `FloaterPose`.\n\n**The animation library (WP 8.4)**: readable key-pose clips (format 1 and 2: parse, print, check, the normalized lens, mirroring, the decode to 36 body points), loaded per set; baked to 30 fps rotation tracks on the skeleton, retargeted onto any build (rotation copy aligned at rest, the hip-height ratio, contact IK) or given to three.js as AnimationClip JSON; played by the clip layer in the animator's clip slot, loops at the ground speed, with shardfall's walk-run switch and strike time.\n`parseClip`, `parseClipSet`, `validateClip`, `poseError`, `poseMeanError`, `clipText`, `normalizedLens`, `mirrorClip`, `decodePose`, `samplePose`, `POSE_POINTS`, `POINT_INDEX`, `bakeClip`, `sampleBaked`, `travelAt`, `BAKE_FPS`, `retargetPose`, `clipJSON`, `hipRatio`, `standingHipHeight`, `restSkeleton`, `restPoints`, `restBuild`, `RETARGET`, `loadClipLibrary`, `clipPace`, `walkRate`, `runOverWalk`, `strikeTime`, `GROUND_SPEED`, `STRIKE`, `ClipLayer`, `CLIP_LAYER`; types `ClipSet`, `ClipSource`, `RestBody`, `ClipWarning`, `Clip`, `ClipKey`, `KeyPose`, `LimbPose`, `ClipEvent`, `ClipContact`, `BakedClip`, `RetargetOptions`, `AnimationClipJSON`, `ClipLibrary`, `ClipOrigin`, `ClipLayerOptions`, `ClipPlay`, `ClipSlot`, `ClipLayerState`, `ClipLayerEvent`, `ClipStepInput`.\n\n**Moves (WP 6.1)**: moves as data in metres (the kind move: my-3d2dge's 24, converted by Appendix C's table), timelines whose events carry leftover time, attacks and combos with hit windows, sweeps and root motion, moves played on a humanoid through its body state (the arm path), moves at a moment for previews, measured hit shapes, the cone helpers and a hit's knockback velocity.\n`defineMove`, `getMove`, `resolveMove`, `MOVE_PHASES`, `LEGACY_FIELDS`, `fromLegacy`, `Timeline`, `Attack`, `Combo`, `moveAt`, `measureMove`, `strikePart`, `strikeSegment`, `inArc`, `inHitShape`, `knockbackVelocity`; types `Move`, `MoveSpec`, `MoveInput`, `MovePhase`, `LegacyMove`, `TimelinePhase`, `TimelineMark`, `TimelineEvent`, `TimelineState`, `AttackState`, `ComboState`, `Sweep`, `HitShape`, `StrikeBody`, `StrikeAnimator`, `ArmState`.\n\n**Reactions and secondary motion (WP 6.2)**: a directional flinch, knockdown, death and the get-up chosen by how the body lies (face up or face down), stepped in the sim; verlet chains (hair, tails) with bone-capsule colliders, a floor probe and a rest shape, a cape of two chains, and the squash spring as a volume-preserving root scale, drawn by an animator layer in the view.\n`Reactions`, `REACTION`, `REACTIONS`, `lyingOf`, `Chain`, `CHAIN`, `Cape`, `Secondary`, `HAIR`, `CAPE`, `TAIL`, `SQUASH`, `squashScale`, `BODY_CAPSULES`, `bodyCapsules`; types `ReactionOptions`, `ReactionState`, `ReactionBody`, `ReactionName`, `Lying`, `ChainOptions`, `ChainTuning`, `BoneCapsule`, `CapeOptions`, `SecondaryOptions`.\n\n**Animation checks (WP 6.6)**: the state matrix (each body state held at each facing, from rest and back to rest) and animCheckBody, which checks a whole body over it: every pose, the same hashes twice, 1/60 s against 1/120 s; and the camera presets sheets of it are drawn from.\n`stateMatrix`, `STATE_MATRIX`, `animCheckBody`, `SHEET_CAMERAS`; types `MatrixState`, `MatrixRun`, `StateMatrixOptions`, `BodyCheckOptions`, `BodyCheckReport`, `AnimCheckName`.\n\n**Actions (WP 8.7)**: multi-beat sequences a body plays, beats of body states, clips, IK targets, flips and squashes with held contact frames, events, flags and cancel windows; the engine's set is data/anim/actions/, which loadActions defines.\n`Actions`, `defineAction`, `getAction`, `loadActions`, `actionCheck`, `ACTION`, `FLIP_AXES`, `EFFECTORS`, `CHANNELS`; types `ActionsOptions`, `ActionsState`, `Action`, `Beat`, `ActionSpec`.\n\n**Clip sets in the registry (WP 8.9)**: the kind clipset, one entry per set of readable clips, which a page or tool registers from data/anim/sets/ (defineClipSet), and the clip layer of an entity kept as plain data, loaded from it at every use (ClipLayerPool), as an actor's component clips holds it.\n`defineClipSet`, `clipSetFromFiles`, `getClipSet`, `clipSetId`, `clipSetIds`, `defineSetBuild`, `CLIPSET_FIELDS`, `CLIPS_FIELDS`, `emptyClipState`, `ClipLayerPool`; types `ClipSetEntry`, `ClipsData`.\n\n**The quadruped rig (WP 13.21)**: a dog on the planted gait for a crowd body (the rig `quadruped`), its state plain data, its pose the joints its body is drawn on, and its rig contract for the anim QA.\n`Quadruped`, `QUADRUPED`, `QUADRUPED_LEGS`, `QUADRUPED_RIG`; types `QuadrupedOptions`, `QuadrupedInput`, `QuadrupedState`, `QuadrupedPose`, `QuadrupedBone`.\n\n**Physics (WP 3.1)**: Rapier behind plain terms. A scene with `physics: true` has a physics world: bodies by id, raycasts, shape casts, overlaps and ground probes, collision groups by name, a level's bodies; presentation reads it through the read-only QueryView.\n`physicsOf`, `COLLISION_GROUPS`, `PHYSICS_SETTINGS`, `addLevelBodies`, `queryViewOf`; types `Physics`, `BodySpec`, `ColliderSpec`, `BodyState`, `BodyChange`, `BodyType`, `Shape`, `Vec3Like`, `QuatLike`, `Hit`, `Ground`, `QueryOptions`, `RayOptions`, `ShapeCastOptions`, `OverlapOptions`, `ProbeOptions`, `GroupName`, `QueryView`.\n\n**Characters (WP 3.2)**: actors moved by the character controller (Rapier's, moved across then down), with an action game's feel: acceleration and skid, coyote time, a jump buffer, a variable jump, a wall jump, a dash, pushing props; the feel meter measures how one handles. A body's reactions and an actor's secondary motion as components (WP 7.8), and its actions (`actOn`, WP 13.11).\n`createCharacter`, `CHARACTER_FIELDS`, `CHARACTER_STATE_FIELDS`, `spawnActor`, `removeActor`, `measureFeel`, `reactTo`, `stepReactions`, `REACTION_FIELDS`, `SECONDARY_FIELDS`, `actOn`, `ACTIONS_FIELDS`; types `Character`, `CharacterState`, `ActorSpec`, `ActorComponent`, `ActorComponents`, `FeelReport`, `FeelOptions`, `AnimatorComponent`, `SecondaryComponent`, `ReactionComponents`, `ActionComponents`.\n\n**Levels v2 (WP 4.2)**: the hall's glyphs (gallery =, stairs ^, 