called right after `connect()`, before the engine starts,** and the card is HTML. On software rendering a\n  copy's warm-up holds the main thread past the 20 s deadline. A slow copy starts its classes late rather than missing the round.\n- **Sessions use `look:session` and `quality=low`:** the full look's filters and toon style tripled boot time on SwiftShader.\n- **The far sea is plain colour;** `texture:water` turned near-black at a distance, so a textured patch follows you.\n- **Terrain heights, not rays, for placing things:** physics queries see nothing during a scene's setup.\n- **Plumb's open eyes are a twin actor** with its own body for two seconds, since a puppet's body is set when it is made. Same for\n  tracksuits: the guest is respawned on `game.begin`. Plumb's closed eyes are tiny dots rather than arcs.\n- **Great Tilt guests** are 9 crowd humanoids plus Gerald, Bex and Nan as actors; all are carried, not simulated, so none can stick.\n- **Attract runs on an action,** not a restart; the save is only written by the page, and never while attract runs.\n- **`AGENTS.md`'s \"ask the owner\":** nobody was there to ask; no binaries or dependencies were added. No git commits in the dev\n  folder, as the plan says.\n- Everything in the plan's cut line was built; nothing was cut.\nE\ncat > labs/game/tools/write-interview.mjs <<'E'\n/**\n * @file Writes exit-interview.json beside the game's HTML from ui/about-data.ts, so the About screen, the reply and the interview\n * agree. Run from the dev folder: node labs/game/tools/write-interview.mjs <output.json>\n */\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { transformSync } from 'esbuild';\n\nconst source = readFileSync('labs/game/ui/about-data.ts', 'utf8');\nconst { code } = transformSync(source, { loader: 'ts', format: 'esm' });\nconst data = await import(`data:text/javascript,${encodeURIComponent(code)}`);\nconst out = {\n  sourceInformation: data.SOURCE_INFORMATION,\n  testingFeedback: data.TESTING_FEEDBACK,\n  engineFeedback: data.ENGINE_FEEDBACK,\n  promptFeedback: data.PROMPT_FEEDBACK,\n  aiHub: data.AI_HUB,\n};\nwriteFileSync(resolve(process.argv[2]), `${JSON.stringify(out, null, 2)}\\n`);\nJSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(process.argv[2]), 'utf8'));\nE\npython3 - <<'E'\np='ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md';s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"<!-- What the engine should add or fix, most costly first. -->\",\"\"\"<!-- What the engine should add or fix, most costly first. -->\n\n- **`input.pressed` should hold a press until the frame has seen it.** `onFrame` reads the last sample only. When a frame runs more\n  than one sim step (any frame rate under 60) a menu or a pause key read in `onFrame` loses presses. The starter's own pause has\n  this bug. I wrote `ui/nav.ts`, reading keys and pads directly. Expected: a per-frame `pressed` for pages.\n- **`input.use(id)` should stick, or say it won't.** `createEngine` calls `mapFor()` at every sample and switches back to a string\n  `actionMap`. Nothing warns. Play simply stops responding, with all menus still working (they read the system layer). The guide and\n  `input.use`'s comment both read as if a page may switch freely. I now pass a function.\n- **Boot cost of a look.** On SwiftShader `post.look=look:retreat` took 74 s to first frame, `look:session` (sky only) 34 s, no look 14 s\n  (`game:round`, about five bodies and ten props). Could pipelines warm lazily, or the warm-up yield, so a hub deadline can fire?\n  `createEngine`'s warm-up holds the main thread, so a `setTimeout` watchdog fires late.\n- **A way to swap an actor's body and build** without removing it, for outfits. I respawn it.\n- **A bodiless prop is a very good moving prop.** It works today (write `sceneProp.x/y/z/q`) but no document says so; `driveBody` is what\n  the docs lead you to, and its velocity shoves whatever is near. Worth a sentence in the guide.\"\"\")\ns=s.replace(\"<!-- Docs, errors, templates or APIs that s